var e={"renal-urology-chronic-kidney-disease":{checked:`2026-10`,notes:{sections:[{title:`Definition and key facts`,points:[`**CKD** is abnormal kidney structure or function present for **more than 3 months**.`,`It needs **eGFR under 60 mL/min/1.73 m2** or a marker of kidney damage.`,`Damage markers include **ACR 30 mg/g or more**, haematuria or casts, abnormal imaging, or a transplant.`,`A single abnormal creatinine is never CKD; the 3-month rule separates it from AKI.`,`In India about **10-17%** of adults have CKD; **diabetic kidney disease** is the commonest cause.`,`**Cardiovascular disease** is the commonest cause of death, usually before dialysis is ever needed.`,`Causes: **diabetes**, CKDu in farm workers, hypertension, glomerulonephritis, NSAIDs and herbal remedies.`]},{title:`Classification that matters`,points:[`Use **KDIGO CGA staging**: Cause, G category from eGFR, and A category from ACR.`,`G1 and G2 count as CKD only when a damage marker is also present.`,`Read risk on the **KDIGO heat map**: G3a A3 carries more risk than G3b A1.`,`Write the diagnosis in full, for example **'CKD G3b A2 due to diabetic kidney disease'**.`,`The **KFRE** 5-year kidney failure risk of 3-5% or more supports nephrology referral.`,`A 2-year KFRE risk over 40% means start planning kidney replacement therapy.`]},{title:`How it happens (pathophysiology)`,points:[`Nephrons are lost, and the surviving nephrons **hyperfilter** to keep total GFR near normal.`,`Angiotensin II constricts the efferent arteriole, causing **glomerular hypertension**.`,`Raised pressure detaches podocytes, so **albumin leaks** and segmental sclerosis follows.`,`Tubular cells exposed to protein release **TGF-beta**, causing interstitial fibrosis and falling eGFR.`,`Less erythropoietin and excess hepcidin cause **normocytic anaemia**.`,`Phosphate retention raises **FGF23**, calcitriol falls and PTH rises, causing **CKD-MBD**.`,`Late features are acidosis, hyperkalaemia and uraemia, usually below eGFR 15.`]},{title:`History in the OPD`,points:[`Early CKD is silent; ask about **nocturia, frothy urine and facial puffiness**.`,`Uraemic symptoms are anorexia, morning vomiting, **pruritus**, hiccups and cramps.`,`Note duration and control of diabetes and hypertension, and any retinopathy.`,`Ask about glomerular clues: cola-coloured urine, rash, joint pains or haemoptysis.`,`Take a full drug history, especially **NSAIDs and herbal 'kidney tonics'**.`,`Ask about farm work in heat, family history of kidney disease, and pregnancy plans.`]},{title:`Examination`,points:[`Measure **seated BP** after 5 minutes' rest, and postural BP in diabetics and the elderly.`,`Record weight and oedema at every visit; weight gain is the earliest sign of overload.`,`Look for pallor, scratch marks and, in advanced disease, **asterixis** and a pericardial rub.`,`Ballotable bilateral kidneys suggest **polycystic kidney disease**; a palpable bladder suggests obstruction.`,`Do **fundoscopy**: diabetic retinopathy supports diabetic kidney disease.`,`Protect the **non-dominant arm veins** from G4 onwards for a future fistula.`]},{title:`Investigations`,points:[`Stage with **eGFR by CKD-EPI 2021**; dose drugs with **Cockcroft-Gault** creatinine clearance.`,`Test albuminuria with an **early-morning urine ACR**; dipstick misses A2.`,`Repeat eGFR and ACR at **3 months** to prove chronicity.`,`**Red cell casts** on microscopy mean glomerulonephritis and need referral.`,`**Ultrasound KUB** in every new case; small echogenic kidneys mean chronic scarring.`,`Baseline Na, K, bicarbonate, calcium, phosphate, Hb, HbA1c, lipids, HBsAg, HCV and HIV.`,`Screen yearly with **ACR plus eGFR** in diabetes, hypertension, CVD, obesity and age over 60.`]},{title:`Treatment - first line`,points:[`Salt **under 5 g a day**; protein about **0.8 g/kg/day** in G3-G5; avoid NSAIDs.`,`BP target is **systolic under 120 mmHg** if tolerated, otherwise under 130/80.`,`Give an **ACE inhibitor or ARB** titrated to the maximum tolerated dose.`,`Accept a **creatinine rise under 30%** after starting; recheck at 2-4 weeks.`,`Add an **SGLT2 inhibitor** (dapagliflozin or empagliflozin 10 mg) if eGFR is 20 or more.`,`Once started, continue the SGLT2 inhibitor **until dialysis or transplant**.`,`Give a **statin** to everyone with CKD aged 50 or more.`]},{title:`Treatment - next steps`,points:[`Add **finerenone** in type 2 diabetes with ACR 30 or more and potassium 4.8 or less.`,`Add **semaglutide** weekly in type 2 diabetes with CKD not at glycaemic target.`,`Give **sodium bicarbonate** when serum bicarbonate is under 18 mmol/L.`,`Use a **potassium binder** for chronic hyperkalaemia so RAS blockade can continue.`,`Anaemia: **iron first**, then an ESA by shared decision, keeping **Hb below 11.5 g/dL**.`,`Phosphate binders are taken **with meals**; correct low vitamin D with cholecalciferol.`,`**Metformin**: maximum 1000 mg a day at eGFR 30-44, and stop below 30.`]},{title:`Complications and red flags`,points:[`**Potassium 6.5 or more, or ECG change**: IV calcium, insulin-glucose, same-day referral.`,`Pulmonary oedema needs IV furosemide 80-120 mg and dialysis assessment.`,`Urgent dialysis for refractory hyperkalaemia, overload, **pH under 7.1**, pericarditis or encephalopathy.`,`Hydronephrosis with rising creatinine is obstruction; catheterise and refer the same day.`,`Haemoptysis with haematuria and rising creatinine suggests **pulmonary-renal syndrome**.`]},{title:`Referral and follow-up`,points:[`Refer at **eGFR under 30**, **ACR 300 mg/g or more**, or rapid progression.`,`Also refer red cell casts, resistant hypertension on 4 drugs, or suspected hereditary disease.`,`**Rapid progression** is a sustained eGFR fall over 5 mL/min/1.73 m2 a year.`,`Monitor eGFR and ACR **1 to 4 times a year** according to heat-map risk.`,`Create an **AV fistula about 6 months** before expected dialysis.`,`Free haemodialysis is available under the **PM National Dialysis Programme** and PM-JAY.`,`**Pregnancy**: stop ACEi, ARB, finerenone, SGLT2i and statin before conception.`]}],tables:[{heading:`KDIGO staging and risk`,columns:[`G category (eGFR)`,`Meaning`,`Risk at A1 (under 30)`,`Risk at A2 (30-300)`,`Risk at A3 (over 300)`],rows:[[`G1 (90 or more)`,`Normal; CKD only with damage marker`,`Low`,`Moderate`,`High`],[`G2 (60-89)`,`Mildly decreased`,`Low`,`Moderate`,`High`],[`G3a (45-59)`,`Mild to moderate`,`Moderate`,`High`,`Very high`],[`G3b (30-44)`,`Moderate to severe`,`High`,`Very high`,`Very high`],[`G4 (15-29)`,`Severely decreased; refer`,`Very high`,`Very high`,`Very high`],[`G5 (under 15)`,`Kidney failure`,`Very high`,`Very high`,`Very high`]]},{heading:`Drugs in CKD`,columns:[`Drug`,`Dose and route`,`Duration`,`Key side effects / cautions`],rows:[[`Ramipril (ACEi)`,`2.5 mg up to 10 mg oral OD`,`Long-term, maximum tolerated dose`,`Cough, angioedema, high K; teratogenic`],[`Telmisartan or losartan (ARB)`,`40-80 mg or 50-100 mg oral OD`,`Long-term`,`High K; never combine with ACEi`],[`Dapagliflozin or empagliflozin`,`10 mg oral OD, start at eGFR 20+`,`Until dialysis or transplant`,`Genital thrush, euglycaemic DKA, eGFR dip`],[`Finerenone`,`10 mg (eGFR 25-59) or 20 mg oral OD`,`Long-term; K at 4 weeks`,`Hyperkalaemia; no strong CYP3A4 inhibitors`],[`Semaglutide`,`0.25 then 0.5 then 1 mg SC weekly`,`Long-term`,`Nausea, pancreatitis; avoid in MTC or MEN2`],[`Atorvastatin or rosuvastatin`,`20 mg, or 10 mg max if eGFR under 30`,`Lifelong`,`Myalgia; do not start de novo on dialysis`],[`Sodium bicarbonate`,`500 mg-1 g oral BD-TDS`,`While bicarbonate under 18`,`Bloating, sodium load, oedema`],[`SZC (K binder)`,`10 g TDS up to 72 h, then 5 g OD`,`As needed to keep RASi`,`Oedema; separate other drugs 2-3 h`],[`Ferrous sulfate, then IV iron`,`200 mg oral OD; FCM 20 mg/kg up to 1000 mg`,`Oral 1-3 months, then IV`,`GI upset; IV low phosphate (FCM)`],[`Epoetin alfa (ESA)`,`50-100 IU/kg SC three times weekly`,`Keep Hb below 11.5 g/dL`,`Hypertension, thrombosis, stroke`]]},{heading:`Anaemia of CKD - KDIGO 2026 cut-offs`,columns:[`Decision`,`Cut-off`],rows:[[`Start iron (non-dialysis)`,`Ferritin under 100 with TSAT under 40%`],[`Also start iron`,`Ferritin 100-299 with TSAT under 25%`],[`Withhold routine iron`,`Ferritin over 700 or TSAT 40% or more`],[`Start ESA`,`Individualised in non-dialysis; dialysis usually Hb 9-10`],[`Hb ceiling on ESA`,`Keep below 11.5 g/dL; never aim for 13`]]}],remember:[`Stage on two axes: G from eGFR, A from ACR.`,`CKD-EPI 2021 to stage; Cockcroft-Gault to dose drugs.`,`eGFR dip up to 30% on ACEi, ARB or SGLT2i means the drug is working.`,`Four pillars: BP, maximum ACEi or ARB, SGLT2i, statin (plus finerenone in T2DM).`,`Small kidneys mean chronic disease, except diabetes, amyloid, ADPKD and HIV.`,`SADMANS sick-day rules: Sulfonylureas, ACEi, Diuretics, Metformin, ARB, NSAIDs, SGLT2i.`,`Low-sodium salt substitute is potassium chloride: dangerous in CKD.`]},updated:[`KDIGO 2026 anaemia: non-dialysis iron start - ferritin under 100 ng/mL with TSAT under 40%, or ferritin 100-299 with TSAT under 25%; withhold if ferritin over 700 or TSAT 40% or more (replaces TSAT 30% or less and ferritin 500 or less)`,`KDIGO 2026 anaemia: ESA start individualised in non-dialysis CKD (no fixed Hb 10 trigger); keep Hb below 11.5 g/dL on ESA`,`KDIGO 2026 anaemia: ESA preferred over HIF-PHI (desidustat, daprodustat) as first-line; IV rather than oral iron in haemodialysis`,`ERC 2025: hyperkalaemia with ECG change - calcium gluconate 10% 30 mL over 10 min or calcium chloride 10% 10 mL over 5 min (was 10-30 mL)`],master:{heading:`Master revision table - Chronic kidney disease (KDIGO 2024)`,columns:[`Aspect`,`High-yield points`],rows:[[`Definition`,`Abnormal kidney structure or function **for more than 3 months** - **eGFR under 60 mL/min/1.73 m2** or a damage marker (**ACR 30 mg/g or more**, haematuria/casts, imaging, histology, transplant). One abnormal creatinine is never CKD.`],[`Relevant classification`,`**KDIGO CGA**: Cause; **G1 90+, G2 60-89, G3a 45-59, G3b 30-44, G4 15-29, G5 under 15**; **A1 under 30, A2 30-300, A3 over 300 mg/g**; risk on heat map (**G3a A3 worse than G3b A1**); **KFRE 5-year risk 3-5%+ = refer**, 2-year over 40% = plan KRT.`],[`Aetiology & risk factors`,`**Diabetes (commonest)**, hypertension, chronic GN, **CKDu** (Uddanam, heat stress, agrochemicals), obstruction/stones, ADPKD, NSAIDs, herbal heavy metals, past AKI (dengue, lepto, snakebite, obstetric), HIV.`],[`Pathophysiology`,`Nephron loss -> **hyperfiltration** -> glomerular hypertension (angiotensin II efferent constriction) -> podocyte loss -> **albuminuria** -> tubular TGF-beta -> **interstitial fibrosis** -> falling eGFR -> anaemia, CKD-MBD, acidosis, hyperkalaemia, uraemia.`],[`Key history`,`Usually silent - **nocturia, frothy urine, puffiness**; uraemic symptoms (anorexia, pruritus, hiccups); diabetes/HTN duration, retinopathy; **NSAID and ayurvedic use**; glomerular clues; LUTS/stones; occupation (farm heat); family history; pregnancy plans.`],[`Key examination`,`**Seated BP** with right cuff and postural BP; weight and oedema; pallor, uraemic fetor, asterixis, rub; JVP and crackles; **ballotable kidneys (ADPKD)**, palpable bladder, renal bruit; **fundus**; feet; protect non-dominant arm veins.`],[`Investigations`,`**Creatinine with eGFR CKD-EPI 2021 (race-free)**; **early-morning ACR, repeat to confirm**; repeat both at 3 months; urine microscopy; **USG KUB** (small echogenic = chronic; large in DKD, amyloid, ADPKD, HIV); Na, K, HCO3, Ca, PO4, Hb, HbA1c, lipids; HBsAg, HCV, HIV; **cystatin C** if creatinine unreliable.`],[`Diagnostic criteria`,`eGFR under 60 or damage marker **persisting over 3 months**; write in full - **'CKD G3b A2 due to diabetic kidney disease'**; rapid progression = **sustained fall over 5 mL/min/year**.`],[`Treatment - first line`,`**Four pillars**: BP **systolic under 120** if tolerated; **ACEi/ARB to maximum dose** (ramipril 2.5-10 mg, telmisartan 40-80 mg; accept creatinine rise under 30%); **SGLT2i dapagliflozin/empagliflozin 10 mg OD if eGFR 20+**, continue to dialysis; **statin** (atorvastatin 20 mg) if 50+.`],[`Treatment - escalation`,`T2DM with ACR 30+ on max RASi and **K 4.8 or less: finerenone 10 mg (eGFR 25-59) or 20 mg**, K at 4 weeks; **semaglutide 0.25 -> 0.5 -> 1 mg SC weekly** (FLOW); chlorthalidone 12.5-25 mg; K binder (SZC 10 g TDS up to 72 h then 5 g OD) to keep RASi; **NaHCO3 if bicarbonate under 18**.`],[`Anaemia and CKD-MBD`,`**Iron first: start if ferritin under 100 + TSAT under 40%, or ferritin 100-299 + TSAT under 25%**; IV if oral fails at 1-3 months; **ESA start individualised, keep Hb below 11.5 g/dL**; ESA before HIF-PHI. Phosphate binders with meals; cholecalciferol for low vitamin D.`],[`Special groups`,`**Pregnancy: stop ACEi/ARB, finerenone, SGLT2i, statin** - labetalol/nifedipine, aspirin 150 mg from 12 weeks; **metformin max 1000 mg at eGFR 30-44, stop under 30**; elderly - relax BP target, deprescribe; hepatitis B **40 microgram 0-1-2-6 months** while eGFR over 30.`],[`Complications`,`**CVD - commonest cause of death**; hyperkalaemia, acidosis, fluid overload, renal anaemia, **CKD-MBD** (FGF23 up, calcitriol down, PTH up), uraemic pericarditis, bleeding, neuropathy, drug toxicity.`],[`Red flags & referral`,`**eGFR under 30**, **ACR 300+**, KFRE 3-5%+, rapid progression, **red cell casts**, resistant HTN (4 drugs), K over 5.5 persistent; urgent: **K over 6.0 or ECG change, pulmonary oedema, pericarditis, encephalopathy** - dialysis.`],[`Follow-up & monitoring`,`eGFR + ACR **1/year (low-moderate risk), 2/year (high), 3-4/year (very high)**; K and creatinine **2-4 weeks** after RASi/finerenone; **AV fistula about 6 months before dialysis**, refer around eGFR 30; sick-day rules (SADMANS).`],[`Prevention / programme`,`**Annual ACR + eGFR screening** in diabetes, HTN, CVD, obesity, family history, age over 60; salt under 5 g, no NSAIDs; **PM National Dialysis Programme** and PM-JAY; CKDu belts - water, shade, rest.`],[`Latest guideline change`,`**KDIGO 2026 anaemia**: new iron start thresholds, ESA start individualised, **Hb below 11.5 g/dL on ESA**, ESA preferred over HIF-PHI; **ERC 2025** calcium for hyperkalaemia 30 mL gluconate or 10 mL chloride.`],[`Exam one-liners`,`Stage with eGFR, dose with Cockcroft-Gault; **eGFR dip up to 30% on RASi/SGLT2i = drug working**; SGLT2i continue below 20; **low-sodium salt = KCl**; small kidneys = chronic except DKD, amyloid, ADPKD, HIV.`]]},flows:[{kind:`flow`,heading:`CKD in the OPD - confirm, stage, protect`,caption:`Condensed KDIGO 2024 pathway from first abnormal result to the drug list.`,steps:[{label:`Abnormal eGFR or ACR found`,detail:`eGFR under 60 or ACR 30 mg/g or more on early-morning sample`},{label:`Repeat at 3 months - still abnormal?`,detail:`Rising over days-weeks = AKI, not CKD`,tone:`decision`},{label:`Stage G and A, find the cause`,detail:`Microscopy, USG KUB, glucose, serology if active sediment`},{label:`Red cell casts, eGFR under 30 or ACR 300+?`,detail:`Refer nephrology; same day if K over 6.0 or overload`,tone:`warn`},{label:`Start the four pillars`,detail:`Max ACEi/ARB, SGLT2i if eGFR 20+, statin if 50+, finerenone in T2DM with ACR 30+ and K 4.8 or less`},{label:`Monitor by heat-map risk`,detail:`1-4 times a year; K and creatinine 2-4 weeks after any RASi change`,tone:`good`}]},{kind:`ladder`,heading:`Anaemia of CKD - KDIGO 2026 ladder`,caption:`Iron before any stimulating agent; never chase a normal Hb.`,steps:[{label:`Exclude blood loss, B12, folate, thyroid`,detail:`Ferritin under 45 or microcytosis - look for GI or gynae loss`},{label:`Iron if deficient`,detail:`Ferritin under 100 + TSAT under 40%, or ferritin 100-299 + TSAT under 25%; withhold if ferritin over 700 or TSAT 40%+`},{label:`Oral, then IV iron`,detail:`Ferrous sulfate 200 mg OD/alternate days; IV iron sucrose or FCM 20 mg/kg if no response in 1-3 months; IV first on HD`},{label:`ESA by shared decision`,detail:`Epoetin 50-100 IU/kg SC thrice weekly; dialysis usually at Hb 9-10; ESA before HIF-PHI`,tone:`decision`},{label:`Keep Hb below 11.5 g/dL`,detail:`Targeting 13 raised stroke and death (CHOIR, CREATE, TREAT)`,tone:`warn`}]}]},"renal-urology-acute-kidney-injury":{checked:`2026-10`,notes:{sections:[{title:`Definition and key facts`,points:[`**AKI** is an abrupt fall in GFR, defined by any one of three KDIGO criteria.`,`Creatinine rise of **0.3 mg/dL or more within 48 hours**.`,`Creatinine **1.5 times baseline or more** within the prior 7 days.`,`Urine output **under 0.5 mL/kg/h for 6 hours**.`,`Damage persisting 7-90 days is **acute kidney disease**; beyond 90 days it is CKD.`,`In India most AKI is community-acquired in young people and often reversible.`,`Stage by **KDIGO 1-3** on whichever of creatinine or urine output gives the higher stage.`]},{title:`Causes and risk factors`,points:[`Prerenal: vomiting, diarrhoea, bleeding, sepsis and the **NSAID-ACEi-diuretic 'triple whammy'**.`,`Tropical: **malaria, leptospirosis, scrub typhus, dengue** and viper snake bite.`,`Toxic: aminoglycosides, contrast, hair dye, copper sulphate and herbal remedies.`,`Obstetric: septic abortion, postpartum haemorrhage, pre-eclampsia, HELLP and puerperal sepsis.`,`Rhabdomyolysis after crush, seizures or long immobility gives pigment nephropathy.`,`**Pre-existing CKD** is the strongest risk factor for AKI.`,`Aetiology splits into **prerenal (40-55%)**, intrinsic renal (35-45%) and **postrenal (5-10%)**.`]},{title:`How it happens (pathophysiology)`,points:[`Autoregulation holds GFR steady between mean arterial pressures of about 80-180 mmHg.`,`Below this, GFR falls with pressure; intact tubules hold sodium, giving **prerenal AKI**.`,`Prolonged ischaemia or toxins kill the **S3 proximal tubule** and thick ascending limb.`,`Shed cells form **muddy brown granular casts** that block tubules.`,`GFR also falls from backleak of filtrate and tubuloglomerular feedback vasoconstriction.`,`Postrenal AKI needs obstruction of **both kidneys** or a single functioning kidney.`,`Creatinine **lags true GFR by 24-48 hours**, so urine output and trend matter more.`]},{title:`History in the OPD`,points:[`Ask about urine volume; complete anuria suggests obstruction, cortical necrosis or vascular occlusion.`,`Ask about fluid loss, bleeding, heat exposure and excess diuretic use.`,`Take a two-week drug history, including **NSAIDs, ACEi, ARB** and herbal remedies.`,`Ask about recent contrast, surgery, fever with rural exposure, and bites or poisons.`,`Ask about poor stream, loin pain, known stones or a blocked catheter.`,`Ask about haematuria, rash, joint pains or haemoptysis, suggesting glomerulonephritis.`]},{title:`Examination`,points:[`Assess volume with several signs: **postural BP**, JVP, capillary refill, weight and oedema.`,`**Palpate for a bladder** and check any catheter; a full bladder needs a catheter first.`,`Look for overload: raised JVP, crackles, S3 and sacral oedema.`,`Uraemic signs are asterixis, **pericardial rub** and Kussmaul breathing.`,`Look for an **eschar** (scrub typhus), conjunctival suffusion (leptospirosis) and fang marks.`]},{title:`Investigations`,points:[`Daily **creatinine and urea**, compared with any old value, stage AKI and show trend.`,`Check **potassium, bicarbonate and an ECG** at once.`,`Urine microscopy: muddy brown casts mean ATN; **red cell casts** mean glomerulonephritis.`,`Dipstick blood without red cells means **myoglobin or haemoglobin**.`,`**Ultrasound KUB within 24 hours** (within 6 hours if pyonephrosis suspected).`,`**FeNa under 1%** suggests prerenal; over 2% suggests ATN; use FeUrea on diuretics.`,`Send malaria, dengue, leptospira and scrub typhus tests by season and story.`]},{title:`Treatment - first line`,points:[`Treat the cause, restore perfusion, **relieve obstruction**, and stop nephrotoxins.`,`Hold the **SADMANS** drugs and all nephrotoxins until recovery.`,`Give **balanced crystalloid** in 250-500 mL boluses, reassessing after each one.`,`In shock give **noradrenaline** peripherally to a mean arterial pressure of 65 mmHg.`,`Never use hydroxyethyl starch, low-dose dopamine or mannitol.`,`Keep a fluid balance chart and daily weight; protein **0.8-1.0 g/kg/day**, never restricted to delay dialysis.`]},{title:`Treatment - next steps`,points:[`Loop diuretics treat **overload only**; furosemide 40-80 mg IV.`,`Under 200 mL urine in 2 hours after 1-1.5 mg/kg furosemide predicts dialysis.`,`Hyperkalaemia: **IV calcium**, then insulin-glucose and salbutamol, then a binder or dialysis.`,`IV bicarbonate only for **pH under 7.2** when not overloaded.`,`Interstitial nephritis: stop the drug; **prednisolone 1 mg/kg** if no improvement in 3-5 days.`,`Hepatorenal AKI: **albumin**, then terlipressin if no response.`]},{title:`Complications and red flags`,points:[`**AEIOU** dialysis indications: Acidosis, Electrolytes, Intoxication, Overload, Uraemia.`,`No urea or creatinine value alone mandates dialysis.`,`Hydronephrosis with fever is an infected obstructed kidney needing urgent drainage.`,`**Tea-coloured urine** after crush or immobility means rhabdomyolysis; check CK and give fluids.`,`After relieving retention, watch for **post-obstructive diuresis** over 200 mL/h.`]},{title:`Referral and follow-up`,points:[`Refer same day for **stage 3**, dialysis need, active sediment or suspected vasculitis.`,`Refer AKI that fails to improve within **5-7 days** of correcting the cause.`,`Review every survivor at **3 months** with creatinine, eGFR and urine ACR.`,`Restart held drugs **one at a time** and record the AKI episode prominently.`,`Avoid NSAIDs in CKD, the elderly and heart failure; treat diarrhoea early with ORS.`,`For contrast, use the least dose and **isotonic saline** if eGFR is under 30.`]}],tables:[{heading:`KDIGO staging of AKI`,columns:[`Stage`,`Creatinine`,`Urine output`],rows:[[`1`,`1.5-1.9 times baseline, or rise 0.3 mg/dL in 48 h`,`Under 0.5 mL/kg/h for 6-12 h`],[`2`,`2.0-2.9 times baseline`,`Under 0.5 mL/kg/h for 12 h or more`],[`3`,`3 times baseline, 4.0 mg/dL or more, or dialysis`,`Under 0.3 mL/kg/h for 24 h, or anuria 12 h`]]},{heading:`Prerenal AKI versus acute tubular necrosis`,columns:[`Feature`,`Prerenal`,`Acute tubular necrosis`],rows:[[`Urine sediment`,`Bland or hyaline casts`,`Muddy brown granular casts`],[`Urine sodium`,`Under 20 mmol/L`,`Over 40 mmol/L`],[`FeNa`,`Under 1%`,`Over 2%`],[`FeUrea (on diuretics)`,`Under 35%`,`Over 50%`],[`Urine osmolality`,`Over 500 mOsm/kg`,`Under 350 mOsm/kg`],[`Response to fluid`,`Creatinine falls in 24-72 h`,`No early improvement`]]},{heading:`Drugs in AKI`,columns:[`Drug`,`Dose and route`,`Duration`,`Key side effects / cautions`],rows:[[`Ringer's lactate or Plasma-Lyte`,`250-500 mL IV over 15-30 min`,`Reassess after each bolus`,`Fluid overload; avoid starches`],[`Noradrenaline`,`0.05-0.5 microgram/kg/min IV`,`To MAP 65 mmHg after fluids`,`Ischaemia, arrhythmia; start peripherally`],[`Furosemide`,`40-80 mg IV, or 5-20 mg/h infusion`,`Only while overloaded`,`Ototoxicity if over 4 mg/min`],[`Calcium gluconate 10%`,`30 mL IV over 10 min`,`Repeat if ECG changes persist`,`Does not lower K; caution digoxin`],[`Insulin with glucose`,`10 units soluble with 25 g glucose IV`,`Single dose, repeat as needed`,`Hypoglycaemia up to 6 h`],[`Salbutamol`,`10-20 mg nebulised`,`Adjunct to insulin`,`Tachycardia; never alone`],[`Sodium zirconium cyclosilicate`,`10 g oral TDS`,`Up to 72 hours`,`Oedema; separate other drugs`],[`Prednisolone (AIN)`,`1 mg/kg/day oral, max 60 mg`,`2-4 weeks, taper over 4-6`,`Hyperglycaemia, infection`],[`Terlipressin with albumin`,`1 mg IV 4-6 hourly; albumin 1 g/kg day 1`,`Stop if no response by day 4`,`Ischaemia, fluid overload`]]}],remember:[`Stage on the worse of creatinine or urine output.`,`Creatinine lags GFR by 1-2 days; a normal value in anuria is a trap.`,`Check the bladder and do ultrasound first: obstruction is the one cause a catheter cures.`,`AEIOU: Acidosis, Electrolytes, Intoxication, Overload, Uraemia.`,`SADMANS: Sulfonylureas, ACEi, Diuretics, Metformin, ARB, NSAIDs, SGLT2i.`,`Calcium protects, insulin shifts, binders and dialysis remove potassium.`,`Diuretics treat overload, not AKI.`,`Every AKI survivor needs creatinine and ACR at 3 months.`]},updated:[`Surviving Sepsis 2026: start noradrenaline peripherally rather than waiting for a central line; initial MAP 65 mmHg (60-65 acceptable at 65 years or older); balanced crystalloid over 0.9% saline`,`ERC 2025: hyperkalaemia with ECG change - calcium gluconate 10% 30 mL over 10 min or calcium chloride 10% 10 mL over 5 min`],master:{heading:`Master revision table - Acute kidney injury (KDIGO)`,columns:[`Aspect`,`High-yield points`],rows:[[`Definition`,`Abrupt fall in GFR: **creatinine rise 0.3 mg/dL or more in 48 h**, or **1.5 times baseline in 7 days**, or **urine under 0.5 mL/kg/h for 6 h**. AKD = 7-90 days; beyond 90 days = CKD.`],[`Relevant classification`,`**KDIGO stage 1** (1.5-1.9x or +0.3; UO under 0.5 for 6-12 h), **stage 2** (2-2.9x; under 0.5 for 12 h+), **stage 3** (3x, creatinine 4.0+, dialysis; UO under 0.3 for 24 h or anuria 12 h); stage by **worse criterion**. Aetiology: prerenal, intrinsic (ATN), postrenal.`],[`Aetiology & risk factors`,`India: **diarrhoea, malaria, leptospirosis, scrub typhus, dengue, viper bite**, obstetric (septic abortion, PPH, HELLP), herbal/hair-dye; sepsis, contrast, **NSAID + ACEi + diuretic ('triple whammy')**, obstruction; CKD is the strongest risk factor.`],[`Pathophysiology`,`Hypoperfusion -> loss of autoregulation -> prerenal (intact tubules, FeNa under 1%) -> prolonged ischaemia/toxin -> **S3 tubule and mTAL necrosis** -> casts, backleak, TGF afferent constriction -> **ATN** -> recovery with polyuric phase.`],[`Key history`,`Urine volume; fluid loss; **drugs in last 2 weeks (NSAIDs, ACEi/ARB, aminoglycoside, PPIs, herbal)**; contrast; fever with rural exposure; bites, poisons; crush/immobility (dark urine); LUTS/colic; haemoptysis, rash (GN); pregnancy.`],[`Key examination`,`**Volume status** (postural BP, JVP, CRT, weight); **palpable bladder and DRE**; overload (crackles, S3); uraemic signs (asterixis, rub); **eschar, conjunctival suffusion, calf tenderness**, purpura, fang marks; compartment tension.`],[`Investigations`,`Creatinine vs old value, daily; **K and VBG + ECG at once**; dipstick + microscopy (**muddy brown casts = ATN, RBC casts = GN, blood without RBC = myoglobin**); **USG KUB within 24 h**; FeNa (under 1% prerenal, over 2% ATN; FeUrea under 35% on diuretics); CBC, smear, CK, malaria/dengue/lepto/scrub tests, 20WBCT.`],[`Diagnostic criteria`,`KDIGO creatinine or UO criteria; **baseline = lowest recent value**; creatinine lags GFR by 24-48 h, so trend and urine output matter more.`],[`Treatment - first line`,`**Relieve obstruction (catheter)**, stop **SADMANS** and nephrotoxins, **balanced crystalloid 250-500 mL boluses with reassessment**, no starch; **noradrenaline (peripheral start allowed) to MAP 65** (60-65 if 65+); dose drugs by CrCl but **full first dose**.`],[`Treatment - escalation`,`Overload only: **furosemide 40-80 mg IV** (1-1.5 mg/kg stress test - under 200 mL in 2 h predicts dialysis); **AEIOU dialysis**: pH under 7.1, K 6.5+ refractory, toxins (methanol, lithium), refractory oedema, uraemic pericarditis/encephalopathy. No benefit from early-by-numbers dialysis.`],[`Hyperkalaemia sequence`,`**Calcium gluconate 10% 30 mL over 10 min or chloride 10 mL** -> **insulin 10 units + glucose 25 g** (glucose hourly for 6 h) + salbutamol 10-20 mg neb -> **SZC 10 g TDS** / binder, diuretic -> dialysis.`],[`Special causes`,`**Rhabdomyolysis**: crystalloid to UO 200-300 mL/h; **AIN**: stop drug, prednisolone 1 mg/kg; **HRS-AKI**: albumin 1 g/kg then terlipressin 1 mg 4-6 hourly; **lepto**: penicillin G or ceftriaxone; **malaria**: IV artesunate 2.4 mg/kg; **viper**: ASV 8-10 vials.`],[`Special groups`,`Pregnancy: septic abortion, PPH, HELLP -> cortical necrosis; child: stage 3 includes eGFR under 35; elderly: low muscle mass masks rise; CKD: smaller rises matter.`],[`Complications`,`Hyperkalaemia, pulmonary oedema, **high anion gap acidosis**, uraemic pericarditis/encephalopathy/bleeding, infection, drug toxicity, **post-obstructive diuresis**, later CKD (risk tripled) and CVD.`],[`Red flags & referral`,`**Same-day nephrology**: stage 3, any dialysis indication, **active sediment / pulmonary-renal syndrome**, TMA, transplant AKI, no improvement in 5-7 days; urology for obstruction; infected obstructed kidney = urgent drainage.`],[`Follow-up & monitoring`,`Daily creatinine, electrolytes, fluid chart, weight; recovery phase - K, Mg, PO4 losses; **review every survivor at 3 months with creatinine and ACR**; restart drugs one at a time; record AKI episode.`],[`Prevention / programme`,`Creatinine before and 2-4 weeks after RASi; avoid NSAIDs in CKD/elderly/HF; **ORS early in diarrhoea**; least contrast, isotonic saline 1 mL/kg/h if eGFR under 30; sick-day card.`],[`Latest guideline change`,`**SSC 2026**: peripheral noradrenaline start, MAP 65 (60-65 at 65+), balanced crystalloids; **ERC 2025** calcium dosing; KDIGO AKI/AKD 2026 update still a public-review draft (KDIGO 2012 stands).`],[`Exam one-liners`,`Stage on the worse criterion; **catheter before blaming anything else**; diuretics treat overload not AKI; **NAC and bicarbonate do not prevent contrast AKI**; calcium protects, insulin shifts, only binders/dialysis remove K.`]]},flows:[{kind:`flow`,heading:`AKI in the first hour - the exam sequence`,caption:`Condensed version: threats first, then cause, then protect the kidney.`,steps:[{label:`K, VBG and ECG now`,detail:`ECG change: calcium gluconate 10% 30 mL or chloride 10 mL IV; K 6.5+: insulin 10 U + glucose 25 g`,tone:`warn`},{label:`Bladder scan or catheter, USG KUB in 24 h`,detail:`Hydronephrosis with fever - nephrostomy or stent within hours`,tone:`decision`},{label:`Assess volume - dry, euvolaemic or overloaded?`,detail:`Postural BP, JVP, CRT, crackles, weight`,tone:`decision`},{label:`Dry: balanced crystalloid 250-500 mL boluses`,detail:`Reassess after each; noradrenaline to MAP 65 if still shocked`},{label:`Overloaded: furosemide 40-80 mg IV`,detail:`No response to 1-1.5 mg/kg in 2 h - plan dialysis`},{label:`Stop SADMANS and nephrotoxins`,detail:`Dose all drugs by CrCl; full first antibiotic dose`},{label:`Recovery - review at 3 months`,detail:`Creatinine and ACR; no-NSAID card`,tone:`good`}]},{kind:`compare`,heading:`Prerenal azotaemia vs ATN vs obstruction - one look`,columns:[`Feature`,`Prerenal`,`ATN`,`Postrenal`],rows:[[`Urine sediment`,`Bland, hyaline casts`,`Muddy brown granular casts`,`Bland, may have blood`],[`Urine Na / FeNa`,`Under 20 / under 1%`,`Over 40 / over 2%`,`Variable`],[`Urine osmolality`,`Over 500`,`Under 350`,`Isosthenuric late`],[`Ultrasound`,`Normal`,`Normal or swollen kidneys`,`Hydronephrosis, full bladder`],[`First action`,`Fluid challenge`,`Supportive, remove toxin`,`Catheter, nephrostomy or stent`]]},{kind:`branch`,heading:`AEIOU - when AKI needs dialysis`,root:`Refractory to medical treatment`,arms:[{label:`A - Acidosis`,tone:`warn`,steps:[`pH under 7.1`,`Bicarbonate not possible - overload or no response`]},{label:`E - Electrolytes`,tone:`warn`,steps:[`K 6.5 or more`,`Or ECG change despite treatment`]},{label:`I - Intoxication`,steps:[`Methanol, ethylene glycol`,`Lithium, salicylate, metformin acidosis`]},{label:`O - Overload`,steps:[`Pulmonary oedema`,`No response to diuretic`]},{label:`U - Uraemia`,tone:`warn`,steps:[`Pericarditis, encephalopathy`,`Seizures, uraemic bleeding`]}]}]},"renal-urology-urinary-tract-infection":{checked:`2026-10`,notes:{sections:[{title:`Classification that matters`,points:[`**Uncomplicated UTI**: non-pregnant woman with a normal tract and no relevant comorbidity.`,`**Complicated UTI**: men, pregnancy, obstruction, stones, catheter, diabetes, CKD or immunosuppression.`,`**Recurrent UTI** is 2 or more in 6 months, or 3 or more in 12 months.`,`**Relapse** is the same organism within 2 weeks, pointing to a hidden focus.`,`**Asymptomatic bacteriuria** is treated only in pregnancy and before mucosa-breaching urology.`,`**CAUTI** needs symptoms; cloudy or smelly catheter urine alone is not infection.`,`**E. coli** is the commonest organism; over half are ESBL producers in India.`]},{title:`How it happens (pathophysiology)`,points:[`Faecal E. coli colonise the vaginal introitus and **ascend** the short female urethra.`,`**Type 1 fimbriae** bind bladder cells, causing cystitis.`,`**P fimbriae** bind renal epithelium, causing pyelonephritis.`,`E. coli hide in **intracellular communities**, seeding later reinfection.`,`After menopause, low oestrogen depletes **lactobacilli** and lets coliforms colonise.`,`Obstruction, residual urine and **catheter biofilm** shelter bacteria and make infection complicated.`]},{title:`History in the OPD`,points:[`**Dysuria and frequency without vaginal discharge** gives over 90% probability of UTI.`,`Fever, rigors, flank pain or vomiting mean **pyelonephritis**.`,`Discharge or external dysuria suggests vaginitis; a new partner suggests urethritis.`,`Ask about antibiotics or admission in the last 3 months, which predict ESBL.`,`Always ask about **pregnancy**; it makes UTI complicated and limits drug choice.`,`In men ask about poor stream, dribbling and perineal pain.`]},{title:`Examination`,points:[`Screen for sepsis with **NEWS2**; do not use qSOFA alone.`,`Feel for suprapubic tenderness and a **palpable bladder**.`,`**Renal angle tenderness** supports pyelonephritis.`,`In men do a gentle DRE; a boggy, tender prostate is acute prostatitis.`,`**Never massage** an acutely inflamed prostate, as it causes bacteraemia.`]},{title:`Investigations`,points:[`**Nitrite** is highly specific; **leucocyte esterase** detects pyuria.`,`A negative nitrite does not exclude UTI (Enterococcus, Staph saprophyticus, Pseudomonas).`,`Dipstick is unreliable over 65 years and in catheterised patients.`,`No culture for a first simple cystitis in a non-pregnant woman.`,`**Culture before antibiotics** in pyelonephritis, men, pregnancy, children, recurrence and catheter.`,`Ultrasound KUB with residual in men, relapse, stones, or pyelonephritis not better in 72 hours.`,`Sterile pyuria: **three early-morning urines for AFB** plus CBNAAT.`]},{title:`Treatment - first line`,points:[`Uncomplicated cystitis: **nitrofurantoin 100 mg twice daily for 5 days**.`,`Alternative: **fosfomycin 3 g** as a single dose at bedtime.`,`Fluoroquinolones and co-trimoxazole are **not empirical** choices in India.`,`Outpatient pyelonephritis: **cefixime 200 mg twice daily**, ideally after one IV ceftriaxone 1 g.`,`Advise fluids, complete voiding and paracetamol for pain.`,`Nitrofurantoin and fosfomycin are bladder drugs; they never treat pyelonephritis or prostatitis.`]},{title:`Treatment - next steps`,points:[`Admitted pyelonephritis: **ceftriaxone 1-2 g IV once daily**.`,`ESBL with sepsis: **meropenem 1 g IV 8-hourly** or ertapenem 1 g daily.`,`Step down to an oral drug after **48-72 afebrile hours**.`,`Complicated UTI with prompt response: **7 days** of an active agent, or 5-7 days of a fluoroquinolone.`,`Use fluoroquinolones or co-trimoxazole only when culture shows susceptibility.`]},{title:`Special groups`,points:[`**Pregnancy**: treat bacteriuria and cystitis 5-7 days, then a **test-of-cure culture**.`,`In pregnancy avoid fluoroquinolones, tetracyclines, and nitrofurantoin at term.`,`**Men**: cystitis 7 days; acute bacterial prostatitis **2-4 weeks** of a fluoroquinolone or co-trimoxazole.`,`Children with febrile UTI: oral cefixime 8 mg/kg/day for 7-10 days.`,`**CAUTI**: replace a catheter in place over 2 weeks, then treat for 7 days.`,`Postmenopausal recurrence: **vaginal estriol** cream restores lactobacilli.`]},{title:`Complications and red flags`,points:[`**Urosepsis**: cultures and IV antibiotics within 1 hour, fluids, and admit.`,`An **infected obstructed kidney** needs emergency nephrostomy or stent.`,`Diabetic not improving at 48-72 hours: CT for **emphysematous pyelonephritis** or abscess.`,`Febrile UTI in an infant under 3 months needs admission.`,`Pneumaturia or faecaluria suggests a **colovesical fistula**.`]},{title:`Referral and follow-up`,points:[`Review pyelonephritis at **48-72 hours**; failure means abscess, obstruction or resistance.`,`No test-of-cure culture after uncomplicated cystitis outside pregnancy.`,`Haematuria persisting after treatment in anyone **over 40** needs cystoscopy.`,`Refer men with residual urine, relapse, stones, obstruction or recurrent CAUTI to urology.`,`Extra **1.5 L of water a day** nearly halves recurrences.`,`Prophylaxis: nitrofurantoin 50-100 mg or cefalexin 125-250 mg at bedtime for **6 months**.`,`**Methenamine hippurate 1 g twice daily** is a non-antibiotic option.`]}],tables:[{heading:`UTI classes and what they mean`,columns:[`Class`,`Who`,`What to do`],rows:[[`Uncomplicated`,`Non-pregnant woman, normal tract`,`Short oral course; no culture for cystitis`],[`Complicated`,`Men, pregnancy, obstruction, catheter, diabetes`,`Culture first, tissue drug, image the tract`],[`Recurrent`,`2 in 6 months or 3 in 12 months`,`Behaviour, vaginal oestrogen, then prophylaxis`],[`CAUTI`,`Catheter in place or removed within 48 h`,`Replace catheter, culture-directed therapy`],[`Urosepsis`,`Organ dysfunction from urinary source`,`Sepsis bundle in 1 hour; relieve obstruction`],[`Asymptomatic bacteriuria`,`Bacteriuria without symptoms`,`Treat only in pregnancy or before urology`]]},{heading:`Significant bacteriuria by specimen`,columns:[`Setting`,`Count (CFU/mL)`,`Note`],rows:[[`Cystitis in a woman (MSU)`,`10^3 of one uropathogen`,`Symptoms matter more than count`],[`Pyelonephritis (MSU)`,`10^4`,`Always culture`],[`Men (MSU)`,`10^4`,`Always complicated`],[`Asymptomatic bacteriuria (women)`,`10^5 in 2 consecutive MSUs`,`Treat only in pregnancy`],[`Catheter specimen`,`10^3 with symptoms`,`Sample new catheter port, never bag`],[`Suprapubic aspirate`,`Any growth`,`Gold standard in infants`]]},{heading:`Drugs in UTI`,columns:[`Drug`,`Dose and route`,`Duration`,`Key side effects / cautions`],rows:[[`Nitrofurantoin`,`100 mg oral BD with food`,`5 days (cystitis)`,`Lung reaction; avoid eGFR under 45, G6PD, term`],[`Fosfomycin trometamol`,`3 g oral, empty stomach at bedtime`,`Single dose`,`Diarrhoea, nausea`],[`Cefixime`,`200 mg oral BD`,`7 days if prompt response, 10 if slow`,`Diarrhoea, C difficile`],[`Ceftriaxone`,`1-2 g IV OD`,`Until 48-72 h afebrile`,`No renal dose change; biliary sludge`],[`Ciprofloxacin`,`500 mg oral BD, only if sensitive`,`7 days`,`Tendon rupture, QT; avoid in pregnancy`],[`Co-trimoxazole`,`960 mg oral BD, only if sensitive`,`3 days cystitis; 14 days prostatitis`,`High K, rash; avoid first trimester, term`],[`Amikacin`,`15 mg/kg IV OD`,`Short course`,`Nephrotoxicity, ototoxicity`],[`Meropenem`,`1 g IV 8-hourly`,`7-14 days; de-escalate`,`Reserve for ESBL sepsis`],[`Estriol vaginal cream`,`0.5 mg nightly, then twice weekly`,`2 weeks, then long term`,`Local irritation, spotting`]]}],remember:[`Uncomplicated versus complicated decides drug, duration, culture and imaging.`,`Nitrofurantoin and fosfomycin are bladder-only drugs.`,`10^3 CFU/mL with symptoms is significant in a woman; reciting only 10^5 loses the mark.`,`Treat asymptomatic bacteriuria only in pregnancy and before mucosa-breaching urology.`,`Every UTI in a man is complicated.`,`Change the long-term catheter before the first antibiotic dose in CAUTI.`,`Sterile pyuria is TB until three early-morning urines say otherwise.`]},updated:[`IDSA 2025: complicated UTI and pyelonephritis with prompt response - 5-7 days of a fluoroquinolone or 7 days of another active agent (was 10-14 days for beta-lactams); 7 days for urinary-source Gram-negative bacteraemia`,`IDSA 2025: oral beta-lactams - optimised dosing, individualised duration; febrile UTI in men with suspected prostatitis 10-14 days`,`Surviving Sepsis 2026: qSOFA not to be used alone for screening (use NEWS2); at least 30 mL/kg balanced crystalloid in 3 h; MAP 65 mmHg`],master:{heading:`Master revision table - Urinary tract infection in adults`,columns:[`Aspect`,`High-yield points`],rows:[[`Definition`,`Symptomatic infection with **significant bacteriuria**: **10^3 CFU/mL** of one uropathogen in a woman with cystitis, **10^4** in pyelonephritis and men, **10^5** classic; catheter specimen 10^3 with symptoms; SPA any growth.`],[`Relevant classification`,`**Uncomplicated** (non-pregnant woman, normal tract) vs **complicated** (men, pregnancy, obstruction, stones, catheter, diabetes, CKD, immunosuppression); cystitis vs pyelonephritis vs urosepsis; **recurrent = 2 in 6 months or 3 in 12 months**; relapse vs reinfection; ASB; CAUTI.`],[`Aetiology & risk factors`,`**E. coli (75-80%)**, Klebsiella, Proteus, Enterococcus, Staph saprophyticus; short female urethra, intercourse, spermicide, menopause, obstruction, catheter (**3-7% per day**), diabetes, pregnancy; **ESBL over 50% in India**.`],[`Pathophysiology`,`Faecal flora colonise introitus -> ascend urethra -> **FimH type 1 fimbriae** bind uroplakins (cystitis) -> **P fimbriae** -> kidney (pyelonephritis) -> intracellular reservoirs -> recurrence; stasis and biofilm make it complicated.`],[`Key history`,`Dysuria, frequency, urgency, **no vaginal discharge** (over 90% probability); fever, flank pain, vomiting = pyelonephritis; LMP/pregnancy; previous episodes and antibiotics in 3 months (ESBL); men - LUTS; sterile pyuria - TB symptoms.`],[`Key examination`,`Temperature, pulse, BP, RR, mental state (**NEWS2**); suprapubic and **renal angle tenderness**; palpable bladder; men - gentle DRE (**never massage acute prostate**); speculum if discharge.`],[`Investigations`,`**Dipstick** (nitrite specific, LE sensitive; unreliable over 65 and catheterised); **no culture for first simple cystitis**; MSU culture before antibiotics in pyelonephritis, men, pregnancy, recurrence, catheter; USG if men, relapse, no response at 72 h; **CT** for gas/abscess; sterile pyuria - 3 AFB + CBNAAT.`],[`Treatment - first line`,`Cystitis: **nitrofurantoin 100 mg BD x 5 days** or **fosfomycin 3 g single dose** (pivmecillinam 400 mg TDS 3-5 days); **no empirical FQ or co-trimoxazole in India**. Outpatient pyelonephritis: **ceftriaxone 1 g IV once, then cefixime 200 mg BD - 7 days if prompt response**.`],[`Treatment - escalation`,`Admitted pyelonephritis: **ceftriaxone 1-2 g IV OD**, pip-taz 4.5 g 6-8 hourly or amikacin 15 mg/kg OD; ESBL sepsis **meropenem 1 g 8-hourly / ertapenem 1 g OD**; step down at 48-72 h afebrile; **cUTI 5-7 days FQ or 7 days other agent** (IDSA 2025).`],[`Special groups`,`**Pregnancy**: treat ASB and cystitis 5-7 days (nitrofurantoin not at term, cefalexin 500 mg BD), test of cure; no FQ/tetracycline. **Men**: cystitis 7 days, febrile with prostatitis 10-14 days, acute prostatitis 2-4 weeks. **Child**: cefixime 8 mg/kg/day 7-10 days. **Elderly**: no treatment of ASB.`],[`Recurrent UTI prevention`,`Extra **1.5 L water/day**, post-coital voiding, stop spermicide; **vaginal estriol 0.5 mg nightly 2 weeks then twice weekly** postmenopause; **methenamine hippurate 1 g BD**; prophylaxis nitrofurantoin 50-100 mg or cefalexin 125-250 mg at night for 6 months, or post-coital.`],[`CAUTI`,`Symptomatic only; **replace catheter in place over 2 weeks before culture and antibiotics**; 7 days (10-14 if slow); no prophylaxis for insertion; remove catheter early; CISC preferred.`],[`Complications`,`Urosepsis, **infected obstructed kidney (pyonephrosis)**, **emphysematous pyelonephritis** (diabetics), renal/perinephric abscess, papillary necrosis, preterm labour, renal scarring in children.`],[`Red flags & referral`,`Sepsis signs - cultures, **antibiotics within 1 hour**, 30 mL/kg crystalloid; stone or hydronephrosis with fever - **emergency nephrostomy/stent**; diabetic not improving at 72 h - CT; pneumaturia - fistula; persistent haematuria over 40 - cystoscopy.`],[`Follow-up & monitoring`,`Pyelonephritis review at **48-72 h**; no test of cure except pregnancy; repeat dipstick for haematuria after treatment in over 40s; review prophylaxis every 6 months.`],[`Prevention / programme`,`**Antibiotic stewardship** (ICMR AMRSN, NAP-AMR): no treatment of ASB, culture-guided therapy, reserve carbapenems; catheter bundles.`],[`Latest guideline change`,`**IDSA 2025**: shorter cUTI courses (5-7 days FQ, 7 days other), 7 days for urinary Gram-negative bacteraemia; **SSC 2026**: NEWS2 not qSOFA alone, balanced crystalloid 30 mL/kg, MAP 65.`],[`Exam one-liners`,`Nitrofurantoin and fosfomycin are **bladder-only drugs**; negative nitrite does not exclude UTI; every UTI in a man is complicated; **sterile pyuria in India = TB until proved otherwise**; change the catheter before the first dose.`]]},flows:[{kind:`flow`,heading:`Dysuria in the OPD - which UTI and which drug`,caption:`Condensed algorithm for the commonest question in family practice.`,steps:[{label:`Dysuria and frequency - vaginal discharge?`,detail:`Discharge or itch - vaginitis or STI, not UTI`,tone:`decision`},{label:`Fever, flank pain, vomiting or sepsis?`,detail:`Yes - pyelonephritis or urosepsis: culture, NEWS2, admit if unwell`,tone:`decision`},{label:`Complicating factor? Man, pregnant, catheter, stone`,detail:`Complicated - culture first, tissue-penetrating drug`,tone:`decision`},{label:`Uncomplicated cystitis: nitrofurantoin 100 mg BD x 5 d`,detail:`Or fosfomycin 3 g single dose; no culture for a first episode`},{label:`Pyelonephritis: ceftriaxone 1 g IV then cefixime 200 mg BD`,detail:`7 days if prompt response (IDSA 2025); FQ only if sensitive, 5-7 days`},{label:`No better at 72 h - image`,detail:`USG or CT for obstruction, abscess, gas; drain if obstructed`,tone:`warn`},{label:`Cured - no test of cure unless pregnant`,detail:`Recheck haematuria in over 40s`,tone:`good`}]},{kind:`ladder`,heading:`Recurrent UTI in women - prevention ladder`,steps:[{label:`Confirm by culture and exclude a cause`,detail:`2 in 6 months or 3 in 12 months; USG + PVR, glucose`},{label:`Behaviour and fluids`,detail:`Extra 1.5 L water/day, post-coital voiding, stop spermicide, treat constipation`},{label:`Vaginal oestrogen if postmenopausal`,detail:`Estriol 0.5 mg nightly 2 weeks, then twice weekly long term`},{label:`Non-antibiotic prophylaxis`,detail:`Methenamine hippurate 1 g BD; cranberry or D-mannose optional`},{label:`Antibiotic prophylaxis 6 months`,detail:`Nitrofurantoin 50-100 mg or cefalexin 125-250 mg at night, or single post-coital dose; review at 6 months`,tone:`warn`}]}]},"renal-urology-luts-bph":{checked:`2026-10`,notes:{sections:[{title:`Definition and key facts`,points:[`**BPH** is DHT-dependent nodular hyperplasia of the prostatic **transition zone**.`,`BPH is histology, **BPE** is a large gland, and **BPO** is proven obstruction.`,`**Male LUTS** is the umbrella term, since symptoms can come from bladder, urethra or kidneys.`,`Histological BPH affects about 50% of men in their sixties.`,`**Symptoms correlate poorly with prostate size**; a median lobe can obstruct a small gland.`]},{title:`Classification that matters`,points:[`**Storage symptoms**: frequency, urgency, nocturia and urge incontinence.`,`**Voiding symptoms**: hesitancy, weak stream, straining and terminal dribble.`,`**Post-micturition symptoms**: incomplete emptying and post-micturition dribble.`,`Grade severity with the **IPSS**: mild 0-7, moderate 8-19, severe 20-35.`,`Add the quality of life question, scored 0-6, at every visit.`]},{title:`How it happens (pathophysiology)`,points:[`**5-alpha reductase type 2** converts testosterone to the more potent DHT.`,`DHT drives stromal and epithelial growth in the periurethral transition zone.`,`The **static component** is the bulk of the gland, shrunk only by 5-ARI or surgery.`,`The **dynamic component** is alpha-1A smooth muscle tone, relieved within days by alpha blockers.`,`The bladder hypertrophies against obstruction, then becomes **overactive**, causing storage symptoms.`,`Late detrusor failure gives rising residual, chronic retention and **hydronephrosis**.`]},{title:`History in the OPD`,points:[`Score the **IPSS** and ask which symptom bothers him most.`,`Rapid onset or age under 45 suggests stricture, neurological disease or cancer.`,`Ask about haematuria, fever, loin pain, bone pain and weight loss.`,`Review drugs: **decongestants, anticholinergics**, antihistamines and evening diuretics.`,`Ask about nocturnal polyuria causes: heart failure, sleep apnoea and diabetes.`,`Ask about **planned cataract surgery** and sexual function before prescribing.`]},{title:`Examination`,points:[`Feel and percuss for a **distended bladder**; a painless mass means chronic retention.`,`Examine the genitalia for meatal stenosis and phimosis.`,`**DRE is mandatory**: BPH is smooth, rubbery, symmetrical with a preserved median sulcus.`,`A **hard, nodular, asymmetric** gland suggests cancer; refer whatever the PSA.`,`Check perianal sensation and anal tone for a neurogenic bladder.`]},{title:`Investigations`,points:[`**Urine dipstick** in every man, for infection, haematuria and glycosuria.`,`A **3-day frequency-volume chart** identifies nocturnal polyuria (over 33% of output at night).`,`**Post-void residual** under 50 mL is normal; over 100-150 mL is significant.`,`**Qmax under 10 mL/s** on uroflowmetry suggests obstruction.`,`**PSA over 1.5 ng/mL** predicts a gland over 30 g.`,`Creatinine when retention or hydronephrosis is suspected.`]},{title:`Treatment - first line`,points:[`**Watchful waiting** and lifestyle for mild or non-bothersome symptoms.`,`Reduce evening fluids and caffeine; teach **double voiding** and urethral milking.`,`Bothersome symptoms: **tamsulosin 0.4 mg** once daily after the same meal.`,`Alpha blockers act in days but do not shrink the gland or prevent retention.`,`Do not recommend saw palmetto; trials show no benefit.`]},{title:`Treatment - next steps`,points:[`Gland over 30-40 g or PSA over 1.5: add **finasteride 5 mg** or **dutasteride 0.5 mg**.`,`5-ARIs take **6-12 months** but halve the risk of retention and surgery.`,`Persistent storage symptoms with residual under 150 mL: add **solifenacin or mirabegron**.`,`LUTS with erectile dysfunction: **tadalafil 5 mg** daily; never with nitrates.`,`Nocturnal polyuria: **desmopressin** at bedtime with sodium checks.`]},{title:`Complications and red flags`,points:[`**Acute retention** is painful; catheterise at once with a 14-16 Fr Foley.`,`Drained volume over 1 L suggests acute-on-chronic retention.`,`Start **tamsulosin** and try without the catheter after 2-3 days.`,`After chronic retention, replace **half to two-thirds** of post-obstructive diuresis.`,`LUTS with saddle anaesthesia or leg weakness needs same-day spinal imaging.`]},{title:`Referral and follow-up`,points:[`Review 4-6 weeks after an alpha blocker and 3-6 months after a 5-ARI.`,`A fall in IPSS of **3 points or more** is a meaningful response.`,`Surgery for refractory retention, **recurrent UTI, bladder stones**, haematuria or renal impairment.`,`**TURP** suits 30-80 g glands; HoLEP suits any size.`,`Counsel that **retrograde ejaculation** follows TURP in 65-75%.`,`Refer urgently for suspicious DRE, raised PSA or visible haematuria.`]}],tables:[{heading:`IPSS severity and management`,columns:[`IPSS`,`Severity`,`Usual management`],rows:[[`0-7`,`Mild`,`Watchful waiting, lifestyle, bladder diary`],[`8-19`,`Moderate`,`Alpha blocker; add 5-ARI if gland over 30-40 g`],[`20-35`,`Severe`,`Combination therapy; consider surgery early`],[`Any, with complications`,`Complicated`,`Retention, UTI, stones, hydronephrosis: surgery`]]},{heading:`Drugs in LUTS and BPH`,columns:[`Drug`,`Dose and route`,`Duration`,`Key side effects / cautions`],rows:[[`Tamsulosin`,`0.4 mg oral OD after a meal`,`Long term; acts in days`,`Dizziness, retrograde ejaculation, floppy iris`],[`Silodosin`,`8 mg oral OD (4 mg if CrCl 30-50)`,`Long term`,`Absent ejaculation; avoid CrCl under 30`],[`Doxazosin`,`1 mg at night, titrate to 4-8 mg`,`Long term`,`First-dose hypotension, falls`],[`Finasteride`,`5 mg oral OD`,`Long term; benefit after 6-12 months`,`Low libido, ED; PSA halved`],[`Dutasteride`,`0.5 mg oral OD`,`Long term`,`As finasteride; no blood donation`],[`Solifenacin`,`5-10 mg oral OD`,`Long term; PVR at 4-6 weeks`,`Dry mouth, retention, confusion in elderly`],[`Mirabegron`,`25-50 mg oral OD`,`Long term`,`Raised BP; avoid over 180/110`],[`Tadalafil`,`5 mg oral OD`,`Long term`,`Headache; never with nitrates`],[`Desmopressin`,`50 microgram sublingual at bedtime (men)`,`Sodium at day 3-7 and 1 month`,`Hyponatraemia; caution over 65`]]},{heading:`Investigation cut-offs`,columns:[`Test`,`Cut-off`,`Meaning`],rows:[[`Post-void residual`,`Under 50 mL normal; over 300 mL`,`Over 300 mL is chronic retention`],[`Uroflowmetry Qmax`,`Under 10 mL/s`,`Suggests obstruction or weak detrusor`],[`PSA as volume marker`,`Over 1.5 ng/mL`,`Gland over 30 g; benefits from 5-ARI`],[`PSA on 5-ARI`,`Halved after 6 months`,`Double the value to interpret`],[`Nocturnal polyuria`,`Over 33% of 24-hour output`,`Medical, not prostatic, treatment`]]}],remember:[`Alpha blockers act in days (dynamic); 5-ARIs take 6-12 months (static).`,`Only 5-ARIs halve the risk of retention and surgery.`,`Double the PSA after 6-12 months on a 5-ARI.`,`Check residual is under 150 mL before an antimuscarinic.`,`Warn every man on tamsulosin about floppy iris syndrome before cataract surgery.`,`After acute retention: tamsulosin now, trial without catheter at 2-3 days.`]},updated:[`Reference updated to the AUA 2026 three-part BPH guideline; EAU 2025 recommendations retained (no change to doses or thresholds confirmed)`],master:{heading:`Master revision table - LUTS and BPH`,columns:[`Aspect`,`High-yield points`],rows:[[`Definition`,`**BPH** = histological stromal-epithelial hyperplasia of the **transition zone**; BPE = enlarged gland; **BPO** = proven obstruction; male LUTS is the umbrella term.`],[`Relevant classification`,`Symptoms: **storage, voiding, post-micturition**; **IPSS 0-35: mild 0-7, moderate 8-19, severe 20-35** plus QoL 0-6; complicated BPH = retention, UTI, stones, haematuria, hydronephrosis.`],[`Aetiology & risk factors`,`Age (50% in 60s, 80-90% in 80s), **DHT via 5-alpha reductase type 2**, metabolic syndrome, obesity, family history; drugs precipitating retention - decongestants, anticholinergics, opioids.`],[`Pathophysiology`,`DHT -> transition-zone nodules -> **static (bulk) + dynamic (alpha-1A tone)** obstruction -> detrusor hypertrophy, trabeculation -> **overactivity (storage)** -> decompensation -> residual urine -> UTI, stones, **chronic retention -> hydronephrosis**.`],[`Key history`,`IPSS + bother; which symptom is worst; haematuria, dysuria, bone pain; **drug list (cold remedies)**; polyuria, oedema, OSA (nocturnal polyuria); neurological symptoms; sexual function; **planned cataract surgery**.`],[`Key examination`,`Palpable bladder; genitalia (meatal stenosis, phimosis); **DRE: smooth, rubbery, sulcus preserved = BPH; hard, nodular, sulcus lost = cancer**; perineal sensation, anal tone; BP, oedema.`],[`Investigations`,`**Urinalysis**; creatinine if retention suspected; **3-day frequency-volume chart** (nocturnal polyuria over 33% at night); **PVR under 50 normal, over 100-150 significant, over 300 chronic retention**; uroflow **Qmax under 10 mL/s obstructed**; PSA after counselling (**over 1.5 = gland over 30 g**).`],[`Treatment - first line`,`Mild/non-bothersome: **watchful waiting** + evening fluid cut, less caffeine/alcohol, double voiding, bladder training. Bothersome: **tamsulosin 0.4 mg OD after same meal** (silodosin 8 mg, alfuzosin 10 mg).`],[`Treatment - escalation`,`Gland over 30-40 g or PSA over 1.5: **add finasteride 5 mg or dutasteride 0.5 mg** (halves retention and surgery; 6-12 months to act); storage with **PVR under 150**: solifenacin 5 mg or **mirabegron 25-50 mg**; ED: **tadalafil 5 mg**; nocturnal polyuria: desmopressin with sodium checks.`],[`Devices / procedures`,`Retention: **14-16 Fr Foley**, start tamsulosin, **TWOC after 2-3 days**; CISC for chronic retention; surgery - **TURP 30-80 g, HoLEP any size, TUIP under 30 g**, simple prostatectomy over 80 g; Rezum, urethral lift preserve ejaculation.`],[`Special groups`,`Elderly: antimuscarinic cognitive harm - prefer mirabegron; **alpha blocker - floppy iris, tell eye surgeon**; 5-ARI - PSA halves (double it), women of child-bearing age must not handle; CKD - silodosin avoid under CrCl 30.`],[`Complications`,`**Acute and chronic retention**, UTI, bladder stones, diverticula, haematuria, **obstructive uropathy and CKD**, post-obstructive diuresis; drug side effects - postural hypotension, retrograde ejaculation, sexual dysfunction.`],[`Red flags & referral`,`**Suspicious DRE or raised age-specific PSA**, visible haematuria, retention with raised creatinine or hydronephrosis, neurological signs, men under 45, flat flow (stricture), failed maximal medical therapy.`],[`Follow-up & monitoring`,`Alpha blocker **4-6 weeks**, 5-ARI **3-6 months**, then 6-12 monthly: IPSS (**fall of 3+ = response**), PVR, creatinine; PSA on 5-ARI doubled.`],[`Latest guideline change`,`**AUA 2026** three-part BPH guideline and **EAU 2025**: no change to drug doses or thresholds; size-based surgery (TURP 30-80 g, **HoLEP any size**); MISTs (Rezum, urethral lift) when **ejaculation preservation** matters.`],[`Absolute indications for surgery`,`**Refractory retention, recurrent UTI, bladder stones, recurrent gross haematuria, renal impairment from BPO**, large diverticula.`],[`Exam one-liners`,`Size correlates poorly with symptoms; **alpha blockers act in days, 5-ARIs in months and alone reduce retention**; check PVR before antimuscarinic; replace **half to two-thirds** of post-obstructive diuresis; saw palmetto = placebo.`]]},flows:[{kind:`ladder`,heading:`BPH drug ladder by problem`,caption:`Step by the dominant problem, not the PSA alone.`,steps:[{label:`Mild or non-bothersome: watchful waiting`,detail:`Evening fluids, caffeine, double voiding, bladder training; review 6-12 months`},{label:`Bothersome voiding: alpha-1 blocker`,detail:`Tamsulosin 0.4 mg OD after the same meal; acts in days`},{label:`Gland over 30-40 g or PSA over 1.5: add 5-ARI`,detail:`Finasteride 5 mg or dutasteride 0.5 mg; 6-12 months; halves retention and surgery`},{label:`Storage symptoms, PVR under 150 mL`,detail:`Add mirabegron 25-50 mg or solifenacin 5 mg; recheck PVR at 4-6 weeks`,tone:`decision`},{label:`Complicated or failed medical therapy: surgery`,detail:`TURP 30-80 g, HoLEP any size, TUIP under 30 g`,tone:`warn`}]},{kind:`flow`,heading:`Acute urinary retention - catheter to trial without catheter`,steps:[{label:`Painful, palpable, dull bladder`,detail:`Painless with large volume = chronic retention`,tone:`decision`},{label:`Catheterise 14-16 Fr, record volume`,detail:`Over 1 L = acute-on-chronic; check creatinine, culture`},{label:`Watch post-obstructive diuresis`,detail:`Over 200 mL/h for 2 h - replace half to two-thirds`,tone:`warn`},{label:`Start tamsulosin 0.4 mg, remove precipitant`,detail:`Decongestant, anticholinergic, constipation, UTI, alcohol`},{label:`Trial without catheter at 2-3 days`,detail:`Alpha blocker raises success about 1.5-fold (to 45-60%)`,tone:`good`},{label:`Failed TWOC, raised creatinine, hydronephrosis`,detail:`CISC or catheter, add 5-ARI, refer for surgery`,tone:`warn`}]}]},"renal-urology-renal-stones":{checked:`2026-10`,notes:{sections:[{title:`Definition and key facts`,points:[`**Urolithiasis** is stone formation anywhere in the urinary tract.`,`About **70-80% are calcium oxalate**; the rest are phosphate, uric acid, struvite and cystine.`,`India has a **stone belt** from Punjab and Rajasthan to Gujarat and Maharashtra.`,`Lifetime prevalence is about 12% in men and 6% in women.`,`Recurrence is about **50% at 5-10 years**, so prevention matters.`]},{title:`Classification that matters`,points:[`Classify by **composition**; it decides radiology, urine pH and prevention.`,`By size: ureteric stones **5 mm or less pass in about 70%**.`,`Stones of 5-10 mm pass in about 50%; over 10 mm rarely pass.`,`Distal ureteric stones pass more often than proximal ones.`,`**High-risk formers** (recurrent, children, solitary kidney, staghorn) need metabolic work-up.`]},{title:`How it happens (pathophysiology)`,points:[`**Low urine volume** and high solute excretion make urine supersaturated.`,`Crystals nucleate, grow and aggregate, often on **Randall's plaques** in the papilla.`,`Inhibitors such as **citrate** and magnesium normally block crystal growth.`,`A stone impacts at the **PUJ, pelvic brim or VUJ**, causing proximal distension.`,`Prostaglandins drive spasm and pelvic pressure rise, which is why NSAIDs work.`,`Obstruction plus infection makes a closed abscess, or **pyonephrosis**.`]},{title:`History in the OPD`,points:[`Sudden, severe **loin-to-groin colic** with vomiting in a restless patient.`,`A VUJ stone gives groin or testicular pain with frequency and strangury.`,`**Fever with colic** means possible infected obstruction, the true stone emergency.`,`Anuria suggests bilateral obstruction or a **solitary kidney**.`,`Ask about fluid intake, outdoor work in heat, gout, bowel surgery and drugs like topiramate.`,`Do a **pregnancy test** in women; consider a leaking **aortic aneurysm** in men over 50.`]},{title:`Examination`,points:[`Record temperature, pulse and BP; fever with tachycardia is infected obstruction.`,`Colic makes the patient **writhe**; peritonitis makes them lie still.`,`Elicit renal angle tenderness; guarding suggests another diagnosis.`,`Feel for a **pulsatile mass** in older men, and examine testes and hernial orifices.`]},{title:`Investigations`,points:[`Urine dipstick shows **haematuria in about 85%**.`,`**Urine pH over 7** suggests struvite; persistently under 5.5 suggests uric acid.`,`**Non-contrast low-dose CT KUB** is the gold standard and shows uric acid stones.`,`**Ultrasound** is first line in pregnancy and children.`,`Check blood count, creatinine, electrolytes, calcium and uric acid in every case.`,`**Send every stone for analysis**; composition drives prevention.`]},{title:`Treatment - first line`,points:[`**NSAIDs** are first-line analgesia: diclofenac 75 mg IM or ketorolac 30 mg.`,`**Paracetamol 1 g** is the alternative, and the choice in pregnancy.`,`Opioids are second line; antispasmodics add nothing to an NSAID.`,`**Tamsulosin 0.4 mg** daily up to 4 weeks for distal stones 5-10 mm.`,`Forced IV hydration does not flush stones out.`,`Safety-net for fever, uncontrolled pain, vomiting or low urine output.`,`**Pregnancy**: paracetamol first; avoid NSAIDs and tamsulosin; stent or nephrostomy if obstructed.`]},{title:`Treatment - next steps`,points:[`Ureteric stone over 10 mm: **ureteroscopy with laser**.`,`Renal stone under 20 mm: **shock wave lithotripsy** or flexible ureteroscopy.`,`Renal stone over 20 mm or staghorn: **PCNL**.`,`Struvite staghorns need **complete clearance** plus culture-directed antibiotics.`,`Uric acid stones dissolve with alkali to **urine pH 6.5-7.0**.`,`Tell the patient the **JJ stent removal date**; a forgotten stent can destroy the kidney.`]},{title:`Complications and red flags`,points:[`**Infected obstructed kidney**: IV antibiotics within 1 hour and nephrostomy or stent within hours.`,`Obstruction of a solitary kidney, or bilateral obstruction with anuria, needs emergency decompression.`,`Pain that suddenly stops may mean complete obstruction; re-image.`,`Do not watch an obstructing stone **beyond 4-6 weeks**; function is lost.`]},{title:`Prevention and programmes`,points:[`Drink for a **urine output of 2.5 L/day or more**, often 3-3.5 L in Indian summers.`,`**Do not restrict dietary calcium**; 1000-1200 mg a day binds gut oxalate.`,`Limit salt, animal protein, cola and oxalate-rich foods.`,`**Potassium citrate** for recurrent calcium oxalate stones and hypocitraturia.`,`**Thiazide** for recurrent calcium stones with high urinary calcium, paired with citrate.`,`**Allopurinol** for hyperuricosuric calcium oxalate and uric acid stones.`]}],tables:[{heading:`Stone types`,columns:[`Stone`,`Share and X-ray`,`Urine pH`,`Key association`,`Specific prevention`],rows:[[`Calcium oxalate`,`70-80%; opaque`,`Acid to neutral`,`Hypercalciuria, low citrate, low volume`,`Fluids, low salt, citrate, thiazide`],[`Calcium phosphate`,`5-10%; opaque`,`Over 6.5`,`Distal RTA, hyperparathyroidism, topiramate`,`Treat cause; thiazide`],[`Uric acid`,`5-10%; lucent on X-ray, seen on CT`,`Under 5.5`,`Gout, obesity, metabolic syndrome`,`Alkali to pH 6.5-7.0; allopurinol`],[`Struvite`,`5-10%; often staghorn`,`Over 7.2`,`Urease producers (Proteus, Klebsiella)`,`Complete clearance, antibiotics`],[`Cystine`,`1-2%; faint`,`Acidic`,`Cystinuria; hexagonal crystals`,`3-4 L fluid, alkali, tiopronin`]]},{heading:`Stone size and site: what to do`,columns:[`Stone`,`Preferred option`,`Comment`],rows:[[`Ureteric under 5 mm`,`Analgesia and watchful waiting`,`About 70% pass within 4 weeks`],[`Distal ureteric 5-10 mm`,`Tamsulosin up to 4 weeks`,`Review at 2-4 weeks`],[`Ureteric over 10 mm`,`Ureteroscopy with laser`,`Passage unlikely`],[`Renal under 20 mm`,`SWL or flexible ureteroscopy`,`SWL poor over 1000 HU`],[`Renal over 20 mm or staghorn`,`PCNL`,`Complete clearance for struvite`],[`Obstruction with infection`,`Emergency nephrostomy or JJ stent`,`Definitive treatment later`]]},{heading:`Drugs in stone disease`,columns:[`Drug`,`Dose and route`,`Duration`,`Key side effects / cautions`],rows:[[`Diclofenac`,`75 mg IM, or 50 mg oral TDS`,`A few days`,`AKI, gastritis; avoid eGFR under 30`],[`Ketorolac`,`30 mg IV or IM (15 mg if over 65)`,`Short course`,`As other NSAIDs`],[`Paracetamol`,`1 g IV or oral 6-hourly, max 4 g`,`As needed`,`Max 3 g if under 50 kg`],[`Tramadol`,`50-100 mg 6-hourly, max 400 mg/day`,`Short course`,`Vomiting, seizures, serotonin syndrome`],[`Tamsulosin`,`0.4 mg oral OD`,`Up to 4 weeks`,`Dizziness, retrograde ejaculation`],[`Potassium citrate`,`30-60 mEq/day in 2-3 doses with meals`,`Long term`,`GI upset, hyperkalaemia`],[`Hydrochlorothiazide`,`25 mg oral BD (or chlorthalidone 25 mg OD)`,`Long term`,`Low K, low Na, high uric acid`],[`Allopurinol`,`100-300 mg oral OD`,`Long term`,`Rash, SJS; HLA-B*58:01 risk`],[`Ceftriaxone`,`1-2 g IV OD with drainage`,`Until culture-guided step down`,`Use meropenem if ESBL or shock`]]}],remember:[`Colic: patient writhes; peritonitis: patient lies still.`,`NSAIDs beat opioids; IV fluids do not flush stones.`,`Tamsulosin for distal stones 5-10 mm; under 5 mm pass anyway.`,`Never restrict dietary calcium.`,`Staghorn with pH over 7.2 means a urease producer, and E. coli is not one.`,`Obstruction plus infection needs drainage; antibiotics alone fail.`,`Pair thiazides with potassium citrate.`]},updated:[`AUA 2026 medical management of kidney stones: water for urine volume 2.5 L/day or more; thiazide offered for recurrent calcium stones with high or relatively high urinary calcium (targeted, not universal); alkali (potassium citrate) for recurrent calcium oxalate stones and calcium stones in children (replaces AUA 2014/2019)`],master:{heading:`Master revision table - Renal and ureteric stones`,columns:[`Aspect`,`High-yield points`],rows:[[`Definition`,`Crystalline concretions in the urinary tract (nephrolithiasis, ureterolithiasis); ureteric stone causes **renal colic**; recurrence **about 50% in 5-10 years**.`],[`Relevant classification`,`By composition: **calcium oxalate 70-80%**, calcium phosphate 5-10%, **uric acid 5-10% (radiolucent)**, **struvite 5-10% (staghorn, urease)**, cystine 1-2%; by size/site: **5 mm or less pass 70%, 5-10 mm 50%**, over 10 mm rarely; high-risk formers.`],[`Aetiology & risk factors`,`**Low urine volume (heat, Indian stone belt)**, high salt and animal protein, low dietary calcium, hypercalciuria, hyperoxaluria (bowel disease), hypocitraturia, gout/metabolic syndrome, dRTA, hyperparathyroidism, UTI with Proteus, topiramate.`],[`Pathophysiology`,`Low volume + high solute -> **supersaturation** -> nucleation on **Randall's plaque** -> growth (low citrate) -> impaction at **PUJ, pelvic brim, VUJ** -> proximal distension + prostaglandin spasm -> colic; obstruction + infection -> **pyonephrosis**.`],[`Key history`,`Sudden **loin-to-groin colic, restless**, nausea; VUJ - strangury, testicular/labial pain; **fever with colic = emergency**; anuria (solitary kidney); previous stones, analysis; diet, fluids, drugs; **LMP**; man over 50 - think AAA.`],[`Key examination`,`Vitals and urine output; writhing vs still (peritonitis); renal angle tenderness; **pulsatile mass and femoral pulses**; testes and hernial orifices; zoster vesicles; distended bladder.`],[`Investigations`,`Dipstick: **haematuria 85%**, pH over 7 struvite, under 5.5 uric acid; CBC, creatinine, Ca, uric acid, CRP; **non-contrast low-dose CT KUB gold standard**; **USG first in pregnancy and children**; strain urine, **stone analysis**; 24-h urine x2 in high-risk formers 4-6 weeks later.`],[`Treatment - first line`,`**NSAID first: diclofenac 75 mg IM or ketorolac 30 mg IV**; paracetamol 1 g IV if NSAID contraindicated or pregnant; opioid second line; no antispasmodics; **MET: tamsulosin 0.4 mg OD up to 4 weeks for distal 5-10 mm**.`],[`Treatment - escalation`,`**Ureteric over 10 mm: URS + laser**; renal under 20 mm: SWL or RIRS; **renal over 20 mm or staghorn: PCNL**; SWL fails over 1000 HU, skin-to-stone over 10 cm; complete clearance for struvite.`],[`Emergency decompression`,`**Infected obstructed kidney**: cultures, IV antibiotics within 1 hour (ceftriaxone; meropenem if ESBL/shock) and **nephrostomy or JJ stent within hours**; also solitary kidney, bilateral obstruction, AKI, intractable pain.`],[`Prevention (AUA 2026)`,`**Water for urine 2.5 L/day or more**; **normal calcium 1000-1200 mg/day**; sodium under 2.3 g/day; less animal protein; **thiazide for recurrent calcium stones with high urinary calcium** (HCTZ 25 mg BD / chlorthalidone 25 mg) + **potassium citrate 30-60 mEq/day**; allopurinol for hyperuricosuria.`],[`Special groups`,`**Pregnancy**: USG, paracetamol, **no NSAIDs after 20 weeks**, no tamsulosin, stent/nephrostomy if obstructed; children - USG, metabolic work-up always; **uric acid - alkalinise to pH 6.5-7.0** (dissolves); cystine - 3-4 L fluid, pH 7.0-7.5, tiopronin.`],[`Complications`,`Pyonephrosis and urosepsis, AKI, **loss of renal function after 2-6 weeks of complete obstruction**, steinstrasse after SWL, **forgotten encrusted JJ stent**, recurrence.`],[`Red flags & referral`,`Fever + hydronephrosis; solitary/transplant kidney; anuria; rising creatinine; **pain that suddenly stops** (complete obstruction); man over 50 with pulsatile mass; woman of reproductive age without pregnancy test.`],[`Follow-up & monitoring`,`Review **2-4 weeks** with imaging; **do not watch an obstructing stone beyond 4-6 weeks**; on prevention drugs repeat 24-h urine at 3-6 months; K, uric acid, glucose on thiazide; home pH strips for uric acid/cystine.`],[`Latest guideline change`,`**AUA 2026** medical management: thiazide targeted to recurrent calcium stones with high urinary calcium; alkali for recurrent calcium oxalate; water for 2.5 L urine/day.`],[`Exam one-liners`,`Colic writhes, peritonitis lies still; **NSAIDs beat opioids; forced IV fluids do not flush stones**; never restrict dietary calcium; **E. coli does not make struvite**; send every stone for analysis.`]]},flows:[{kind:`flow`,heading:`Renal colic - emergency room to discharge`,caption:`Exam version: analgesia, exclude the dangerous, then size and site decide.`,steps:[{label:`Loin-to-groin colic, restless`,detail:`Pregnancy test in women; AAA in men over 50`,tone:`decision`},{label:`Analgesia: diclofenac 75 mg IM`,detail:`Paracetamol 1 g IV if NSAID contraindicated; opioid second line`},{label:`Fever, solitary kidney, anuria or AKI?`,detail:`IV antibiotics within 1 h + nephrostomy or JJ stent within hours`,tone:`warn`},{label:`Low-dose non-contrast CT KUB`,detail:`USG first in pregnancy and children`,tone:`decision`},{label:`Distal 5-10 mm: tamsulosin 0.4 mg up to 4 weeks`,detail:`Under 5 mm usually passes; over 10 mm - ureteroscopy`},{label:`Review at 2-4 weeks with imaging`,detail:`Strain urine, analyse stone; never watch beyond 4-6 weeks`,tone:`good`}]},{kind:`ladder`,heading:`Preventing the next stone - AUA 2026 ladder`,steps:[{label:`Water for urine 2.5 L/day or more`,detail:`Pale urine; glass at bedtime; 3-3.5 L intake in Indian summers`},{label:`Diet`,detail:`Normal calcium 1000-1200 mg; sodium under 2.3 g; less animal protein; more fruit and vegetables`},{label:`Stone analysis and metabolic work-up`,detail:`High-risk or recurrent formers: two 24-h urines 4-6 weeks after the episode`,tone:`decision`},{label:`Targeted drugs`,detail:`Thiazide if recurrent calcium stones with high urinary calcium; potassium citrate 30-60 mEq/day for recurrent CaOx/hypocitraturia; allopurinol 100-300 mg for hyperuricosuria`},{label:`Recheck 24-h urine at 3-6 months`,detail:`K, uric acid, glucose on thiazide; yearly thereafter`,tone:`good`}]}]},"renal-urology-hyponatraemia-hyperkalaemia":{checked:`2026-10`,notes:{sections:[{title:`Definition and key facts`,points:[`**Hyponatraemia** is serum sodium **under 135 mmol/L**.`,`It is almost always a disorder of **water (ADH)**, not of salt.`,`**Hyperkalaemia** is serum potassium **over 5.5 mmol/L**.`,`Potassium **6.5 or more, or any ECG change**, is an emergency.`,`Hyponatraemia is the commonest electrolyte disorder in hospital and in the elderly.`]},{title:`Classification that matters`,points:[`Sodium: **mild 130-134, moderate 125-129, profound under 125** mmol/L.`,`**Acute** is under 48 hours; unknown duration is treated as **chronic**.`,`Symptoms, not the number, decide hypertonic saline.`,`Hypotonic hyponatraemia is classed as hypovolaemic, euvolaemic or hypervolaemic.`,`Potassium: **mild 5.5-5.9, moderate 6.0-6.4, severe 6.5 or more**.`,`Exclude **pseudohyponatraemia** and hyperglycaemia before the work-up.`]},{title:`Causes and risk factors`,points:[`**Thiazides**, especially in elderly thin women within 2-4 weeks of starting.`,`**SIADH** from pneumonia, TB, CNS disease, lung cancer, pain and drugs.`,`SIADH drugs include SSRIs, carbamazepine, antipsychotics and opioids.`,`Hypothyroidism, adrenal insufficiency, beer potomania and tea-and-toast diets.`,`Hyperkalaemia drugs: **ACEi, ARB, spironolactone, trimethoprim** and NSAIDs.`,`**Low-sodium salt substitute** is potassium chloride, a hidden potassium load in CKD.`]},{title:`How it happens (pathophysiology)`,points:[`ADH acts on **V2 receptors** to insert aquaporin-2 and retain water.`,`A 5-10% fall in effective volume releases ADH even when sodium is low.`,`In **SIADH**, ADH is released without stimulus, so urine sodium stays over 30.`,`Water enters brain cells, causing **cerebral oedema**; the brain adapts within 48 hours.`,`Rapid correction after adaptation causes **osmotic demyelination** 2-6 days later.`,`High potassium makes the resting membrane less negative, slowing conduction.`,`**Calcium** raises the threshold potential; insulin and beta-2 agonists shift potassium into cells.`]},{title:`History in the OPD`,points:[`Ask about headache, vomiting, confusion and seizures in acute hyponatraemia.`,`Chronic mild hyponatraemia causes **unsteadiness, falls and fractures** in the elderly.`,`Ask about vomiting, diarrhoea and the fluids drunk or given.`,`Ask about cough, haemoptysis, weight loss and smoking for SIADH causes.`,`Ask about salt craving, pigmentation and stopped steroids for adrenal insufficiency.`,`Hyperkalaemia is often silent; ask about palpitations, weakness and diet.`]},{title:`Examination`,points:[`**Volume status** is the pivot: postural BP, JVP, mucosae, oedema and weight.`,`Record **GCS** and orientation; severe symptoms need 3% saline now.`,`Look for **pigmentation** of palmar creases and buccal mucosa (Addison disease).`,`Look for hypothyroid signs, chest signs, liver disease and heart failure.`,`In hyperkalaemia check pulse rhythm and muscle power.`]},{title:`Investigations`,points:[`Step 1: **serum osmolality**; under 275 mOsm/kg confirms hypotonic hyponatraemia.`,`Step 2: **urine osmolality**; 100 or less means ADH is suppressed.`,`Step 3: **urine sodium**; 30 or less suggests low effective volume.`,`Check **TSH and 8 am cortisol** before calling it SIADH.`,`Hyperkalaemia: **12-lead ECG at once** for potassium 6.0 or more.`,`Repeat an unexpected high potassium with a clean, non-haemolysed sample.`,`A normal ECG does not exclude dangerous hyperkalaemia.`]},{title:`Treatment - first line`,points:[`Severe symptoms: **3% saline 150 mL over 20 minutes**, repeated up to twice.`,`Stop once sodium rises **5 mmol/L** or symptoms improve.`,`Correct chronic hyponatraemia by **no more than 10 mmol/L in 24 hours**.`,`In high-risk patients limit to **8 mmol/L** and aim for 4-6.`,`Hypovolaemic: 0.9% saline; SIADH: **fluid restriction 500-1000 mL/day**.`,`**Never give 0.9% saline in SIADH**; the kidney keeps the water.`,`Stop a culprit thiazide permanently; never rechallenge.`]},{title:`Treatment - next steps`,points:[`SIADH resistant to restriction: **oral urea 15-30 g/day** or salt with furosemide.`,`**Tolvaptan 15 mg** daily, started in hospital, for under 30 days.`,`Overcorrection: **5% dextrose with desmopressin 2 micrograms** to re-lower sodium.`,`Hyperkalaemia step 1: **IV calcium** if ECG changes.`,`Step 2: **insulin 10 units with 25 g glucose**, plus salbutamol 10-20 mg nebulised.`,`Step 3: remove with **SZC**, loop diuretic or dialysis.`,`Do not abandon RAAS blockade for potassium 5.0-5.9 in CKD; use diet and a binder.`]},{title:`Complications and red flags`,points:[`Seizures or coma with low sodium: 3% saline now, regardless of chronicity.`,`Urine output over 100 mL/h signals aquaresis and **imminent overcorrection**.`,`Hypotension, high potassium and pigmentation: Addisonian crisis; **hydrocortisone 100 mg IV**.`,`Cerebral salt wasting after subarachnoid haemorrhage must not be fluid-restricted.`,`Hyperkalaemic arrest: ALS plus calcium chloride 10 mL and sodium bicarbonate 50 mmol IV.`,`Admit symptomatic sodium under 125, sodium under 120, or potassium 6.0 or more.`,`Check potassium and creatinine **2-4 weeks** after starting ACEi, ARB or MRA.`]}],tables:[{heading:`Hypotonic hyponatraemia by volume and urine tests`,columns:[`Type`,`Urine osmolality`,`Urine sodium`,`Causes`,`Treatment`],rows:[[`Low solute or polydipsia`,`100 or less`,`Often low`,`Polydipsia, beer potomania, tea-and-toast`,`Restrict water, restore solute`],[`Hypovolaemic, extrarenal`,`Over 100`,`30 or less`,`Vomiting, diarrhoea, burns`,`0.9% saline`],[`Hypovolaemic, renal`,`Over 100`,`Over 30`,`Thiazides, adrenal insufficiency`,`Stop diuretic; saline; hydrocortisone`],[`Euvolaemic`,`Over 100`,`Over 30`,`SIADH, hypothyroidism, drugs`,`Treat cause; fluid restriction`],[`Hypervolaemic`,`Over 100`,`Usually 30 or less`,`Heart failure, cirrhosis, nephrotic`,`Restrict fluid and salt; loop diuretic`]]},{heading:`Hyperkalaemia severity`,columns:[`Severity`,`Potassium (mmol/L)`,`Action`],rows:[[`Mild`,`5.5-5.9`,`Treat cause, review drugs; recheck in 3-7 days`],[`Moderate`,`6.0-6.4`,`Same-day ECG and assessment; binder`],[`Severe`,`6.5 or more`,`Emergency: calcium, insulin-glucose, salbutamol, SZC`],[`Any level with ECG change`,`Peaked T, wide QRS, sine wave`,`IV calcium first`]]},{heading:`Drugs for sodium and potassium`,columns:[`Drug`,`Dose and route`,`Duration`,`Key side effects / cautions`],rows:[[`3% hypertonic saline`,`150 mL IV over 20 min`,`Up to 3 boluses until sodium up 5`,`Overcorrection, osmotic demyelination`],[`Tolvaptan`,`15 mg oral OD, up to 30-60 mg`,`Under 30 days; start in hospital`,`Overcorrection, liver injury`],[`Oral urea`,`15-30 g/day in juice`,`While SIADH persists`,`Bitter taste, GI upset`],[`Calcium gluconate 10%`,`30 mL IV over 10 min`,`Repeat if ECG unchanged`,`Does not lower K; caution digoxin`],[`Calcium chloride 10%`,`10 mL IV over 5 min`,`Repeat if ECG unchanged`,`Large vein; extravasation injury`],[`Insulin with glucose`,`10 units with 25 g glucose IV`,`Lasts 4-6 hours`,`Hypoglycaemia; hourly glucose 6 h`],[`Salbutamol`,`10-20 mg nebulised`,`Adjunct only`,`Tachycardia; avoid in tachyarrhythmia`],[`Sodium zirconium cyclosilicate`,`10 g TDS, then 5 g OD`,`Up to 72 h, then maintenance`,`Oedema, hypokalaemia`],[`Patiromer`,`8.4 g oral OD, up to 25.2 g`,`Long term with RAAS blockade`,`Low magnesium; separate drugs 3 h`],[`Desmopressin (overcorrection)`,`2 micrograms IV or SC 6-8 hourly`,`With 5% dextrose`,`Sodium every 2 hours`]]}],remember:[`Hyponatraemia work-up: osmolality, then volume, then urine osmolality and sodium.`,`Correction ceiling: 10 mmol/L in 24 hours, 8 in high risk.`,`Saline worsens SIADH.`,`Exclude hypothyroidism, adrenal insufficiency and diuretics before calling it SIADH.`,`Hyperkalaemia: protect (calcium), shift (insulin, salbutamol), remove (binder, dialysis).`,`Repeat an unexpected high potassium with a clean sample.`,`Add 1.6-2.4 mmol/L sodium per 100 mg/dL glucose above 100.`]},updated:[`ERC 2025: membrane stabilisation - calcium chloride 10% 10 mL IV over 5 min first, or calcium gluconate 10% 30 mL over 10 min`,`ERC 2025: no routine IV sodium bicarbonate for hyperkalaemia outside cardiac arrest; in hyperkalaemic arrest give calcium chloride 10 mL and sodium bicarbonate 50 mmol`,`KDIGO 2024: oral bicarbonate threshold for CKD acidosis corrected to bicarbonate under 18 mmol/L (was under 22) to match the CKD topic`],master:{heading:`Master revision table - Hyponatraemia and hyperkalaemia`,columns:[`Aspect`,`High-yield points`],rows:[[`Definition`,`**Hyponatraemia: Na under 135** (mild 130-134, moderate 125-129, **profound under 125**); **hyperkalaemia: K over 5.5** (mild 5.5-5.9, moderate 6.0-6.4, **severe 6.5+**; **any ECG change = emergency**).`],[`Relevant classification`,`Hyponatraemia: acute (under 48 h) vs **chronic (48 h+ or unknown)**; moderately severe vs **severe symptoms**; **hypotonic by volume + urine Osm + urine Na**. Hyperkalaemia: pseudo, reduced excretion, shift, load.`],[`Aetiology & risk factors`,`Na: **thiazides (elderly thin women)**, SSRIs, carbamazepine, SIADH (pneumonia, TB, CNS, SCLC), GI loss with hypotonic fluid, HF, cirrhosis, hypothyroid, adrenal insufficiency, beer potomania. K: **CKD/AKI + ACEi/ARB/MRA**, trimethoprim, NSAIDs, **KCl salt substitute**, DKA, rhabdo, type 4 RTA.`],[`Pathophysiology`,`Na: ADH (V2 -> aquaporin-2) retains water -> dilution -> **cerebral oedema** -> brain adapts in 48 h -> fast correction -> **osmotic demyelination**. K: high ECF K -> less negative RMP -> Na channel inactivation -> **peaked T, wide QRS, sine wave, VF**.`],[`Key history`,`Na: headache, vomiting, confusion, seizures; falls, attention in chronic; drugs; fluids; cough, TB, smoking; Addison features. K: palpitations, weakness; **drug list**, salt substitute, coconut water, CKD, intercurrent vomiting.`],[`Key examination`,`**Volume status** (postural BP, JVP, oedema, ascites) - pivot of the algorithm; GCS; pigmentation (Addison); hypothyroid signs; chest. K: pulse rhythm, power, uraemic signs, urine output.`],[`Investigations`,`Na: **serum Osm** (exclude pseudo and hyperglycaemia - add 1.6-2.4 per 100 mg/dL glucose) -> **urine Osm (100 or less = polydipsia/low solute)** -> **urine Na (30 or less = low effective volume)**; TSH, 8 am cortisol before SIADH. K: **ECG now**, repeat clean sample/VBG, creatinine, bicarbonate, glucose, CK.`],[`Diagnostic criteria`,`**SIADH**: hypotonic, euvolaemic, urine Osm over 100, urine Na over 30, normal thyroid, adrenal, kidney, no diuretic; low urea and uric acid support.`],[`Treatment - first line (Na)`,`**Severe symptoms: 3% saline 150 mL over 20 min, repeat up to 2 more times until Na up 5**; moderately severe: single 150 mL bolus; hypovolaemic: 0.9% saline; SIADH: **fluid restriction 500-1000 mL/day**; stop offending drug; **limit rise to 10 in 24 h (8 if high risk, aim 4-6)**.`],[`Treatment - escalation (Na)`,`SIADH failing restriction: **oral urea 15-30 g/day**, salt 2-3 g + furosemide 20-40 mg, **tolvaptan 15 mg (hospital start, under 30 days)**; overcorrection: **5% dextrose 3 mL/kg/h + desmopressin 2 microgram IV 6-8 hourly**; adrenal: hydrocortisone 100 mg IV.`],[`Treatment - hyperkalaemia`,`**Protect: calcium chloride 10% 10 mL over 5 min or gluconate 10% 30 mL over 10 min** -> **shift: insulin 10 units + glucose 25 g** (10% glucose 50 mL/h x 5 h if glucose under 7 mmol/L) + **salbutamol 10-20 mg neb** -> **remove: SZC 10 g TDS up to 72 h**, patiromer, furosemide, **dialysis**; no routine IV bicarbonate outside arrest.`],[`Special groups`,`Elderly: thiazide hyponatraemia, desmopressin risk; alcoholics, malnourished, hypokalaemic - highest ODS risk; **cerebral salt wasting after SAH - do not restrict fluids**; CKD - keep RASi with binder; digoxin - give calcium slowly.`],[`Complications`,`Seizures, coma, herniation; **osmotic demyelination 2-6 days after overcorrection** (dysarthria, quadriparesis, locked-in); falls and fractures; hyperkalaemic arrhythmia, arrest; insulin hypoglycaemia.`],[`Red flags & referral`,`Na with seizures/coma; **rise over 10 in 24 h or brisk dilute diuresis**; Na under 120; Addisonian features; K 6.5+ or ECG change; K with oliguria; bradycardia on beta-blocker/digoxin.`],[`Follow-up & monitoring`,`Na during boluses every 20 min, then 4-6 hourly; **write the 24-h target on the chart**; Na 1-2 weeks after thiazide/SSRI in elderly; **K and creatinine 2-4 weeks after RASi, MRA (finerenone at 4 weeks)**; repeat K at 1, 2, 4, 6 h after acute treatment.`],[`Latest guideline change`,`**ERC 2025**: calcium chloride 10 mL (or gluconate 30 mL) first; **no routine bicarbonate outside arrest**; arrest - calcium chloride + bicarbonate 50 mmol; hyponatraemia still ESE 2014.`],[`Exam one-liners`,`Hyponatraemia is a **water problem**; **saline worsens SIADH**; never rechallenge a thiazide; **calcium protects, insulin shifts, only binders or dialysis remove**; normal ECG does not exclude dangerous K; low-sodium salt = KCl.`]]},flows:[{kind:`flow`,heading:`Hyponatraemia - symptoms first, then the three tests`,caption:`Condensed ESE pathway: severe symptoms are treated before any test result.`,steps:[{label:`Severe symptoms? Seizure, coma, vomiting`,detail:`3% saline 150 mL over 20 min, up to 3 times until Na up 5`,tone:`warn`},{label:`Serum osmolality under 275?`,detail:`Normal - pseudo; high - glucose or mannitol`,tone:`decision`},{label:`Urine osmolality 100 or less?`,detail:`Polydipsia, beer potomania, tea-and-toast`,tone:`decision`},{label:`Urine Na 30 or less?`,detail:`GI loss, HF, cirrhosis - low effective volume`,tone:`decision`},{label:`Urine Na over 30: diuretic, adrenal, SIADH`,detail:`TSH and 8 am cortisol before calling it SIADH`},{label:`Correct no more than 10 mmol/L in 24 h`,detail:`8 if high risk; overcorrection - dextrose + desmopressin`,tone:`good`}]},{kind:`ladder`,heading:`Hyperkalaemia - protect, shift, remove (ERC 2025)`,steps:[{label:`Confirm and ECG`,detail:`Clean sample or VBG K; ECG change at any level = emergency`,tone:`decision`},{label:`Protect the heart`,detail:`Calcium chloride 10% 10 mL over 5 min or gluconate 10% 30 mL over 10 min; repeat if ECG unchanged`,tone:`warn`},{label:`Shift into cells`,detail:`Insulin 10 units + glucose 25 g IV; salbutamol 10-20 mg neb; glucose hourly for 6 h`},{label:`Remove from the body`,detail:`SZC 10 g TDS up to 72 h, patiromer 8.4 g, furosemide 40-80 mg; dialysis if refractory or oliguric`},{label:`Prevent recurrence`,detail:`Stop KCl salt, NSAIDs, trimethoprim; keep RASi with binder; recheck in 1 week`,tone:`good`}]}]},"renal-urology-drug-dosing-renal-impairment":{checked:`2026-10`,notes:{sections:[{title:`Definition and key facts`,points:[`Drugs with **over about 30% renal elimination** of active drug need dose adjustment.`,`**Stage** CKD with eGFR (CKD-EPI 2021); **dose** with Cockcroft-Gault creatinine clearance.`,`**CrCl = (140 - age) x weight / (72 x creatinine)**, x 0.85 if female.`,`In obesity use **ideal or adjusted body weight**, not actual weight.`,`If only eGFR is available, **de-index** it: eGFR x BSA / 1.73.`,`A 'normal' creatinine of 1.0 in a frail 80-year-old woman may mean CrCl about 30.`]},{title:`Classification that matters`,points:[`The four dosing actions: **reduce dose, lengthen interval, avoid, or monitor levels**.`,`Always ask whether the drug is also **nephrotoxic** or removed by dialysis.`,`Concentration-dependent killers (aminoglycosides): **keep the dose, lengthen the interval**.`,`Time-dependent killers (beta-lactams, vancomycin): **keep the interval, reduce the dose**.`,`Narrow therapeutic index drugs need the most accurate estimate, such as cystatin C.`]},{title:`How it happens (pathophysiology)`,points:[`Falling GFR reduces filtration of unbound, small drugs such as aminoglycosides and lithium.`,`Half-life lengthens, so toxicity may appear days after starting.`,`The **loading dose depends on volume of distribution**, not clearance, so it stays full.`,`**Active metabolites accumulate**: morphine glucuronides, norpethidine and glibenclamide metabolites.`,`Low albumin raises free **phenytoin**, so a low total level may be therapeutic.`,`**Trimethoprim and cimetidine** block creatinine secretion, raising creatinine without kidney harm.`]},{title:`History in the OPD`,points:[`Take a full list, including **over-the-counter painkillers** and 'body pain' tablets.`,`Ask about ayurvedic or unlabelled **'kidney tonics'**, without judgement.`,`Note CKD stage, last creatinine with date, and dialysis or transplant status.`,`Ask about vomiting, diarrhoea or fever, which need sick-day rules.`,`Ask about accumulation symptoms: drowsiness, myoclonus, hypoglycaemia, bleeding or tinnitus.`,`**Weigh the patient** at every visit; weight enters Cockcroft-Gault.`,`**Muscle wasting** means creatinine overestimates GFR.`]},{title:`Investigations`,points:[`Creatinine with eGFR and a calculated **CrCl** before any renally cleared drug.`,`Use **cystatin C** when muscle mass is extreme or near a dose threshold.`,`Potassium **2-4 weeks** after starting ACEi, ARB, MRA or finerenone.`,`Gentamicin **trough under 1 mg/L**; vancomycin AUC 400-600.`,`Digoxin 0.5-0.9 ng/mL; lithium **0.4-0.8 mmol/L** at 12 hours.`,`In evolving AKI, every calculated clearance is an overestimate.`]},{title:`Treatment - first line`,points:[`**Stop NSAIDs** in CKD, heart failure, cirrhosis and the elderly; give a no-NSAID card.`,`Give written **sick-day rules (SADMANS)** to every CKD, diabetes or heart failure patient.`,`**Write the CrCl and its date** on every prescription.`,`Review every renally dosed drug at each visit and after any AKI.`,`Avoid the 'triple whammy' of ACEi or ARB, diuretic and NSAID.`,`**Never reduce the first antibiotic dose** in sepsis because creatinine is high.`]},{title:`Treatment - next steps`,points:[`**Metformin**: full dose at eGFR 45 or more, maximum 1000 mg at 30-44, stop under 30.`,`**Sitagliptin** 100, 50 or 25 mg by eGFR; **linagliptin** needs no change.`,`Avoid **glibenclamide**; use low-dose gliclazide or glipizide with glucose checks.`,`Avoid morphine and pethidine; prefer **fentanyl or buprenorphine** in CKD 4-5.`,`**Enoxaparin** 1 mg/kg once daily if CrCl under 30; heparin in dialysis.`,`**Apixaban 2.5 mg BD** if CrCl 15-29, or two of age 80+, weight 60 kg or less, creatinine 1.5+.`,`Avoid **nitrofurantoin** under eGFR 45 and **dabigatran** under CrCl 30.`]},{title:`Special groups`,points:[`**Haemodialysis**: dose as for CrCl under 10; give dialysable drugs after the session.`,`**CRRT** clears 25-50 mL/min, so antibiotic doses are often higher.`,`**Transplant**: rifampicin lowers tacrolimus; clarithromycin and azoles raise it.`,`**Pregnancy** raises GFR by 40-50%, so creatinine of 1.0 mg/dL is abnormal.`,`Anti-TB: **pyrazinamide and ethambutol thrice weekly** if CrCl under 30.`,`Switch **tenofovir disoproxil to TAF** when CrCl falls.`]},{title:`Complications and red flags`,points:[`Metformin during vomiting or sepsis risks **lactic acidosis**.`,`Tinnitus or vertigo on an aminoglycoside: stop and check the trough.`,`Confusion or myoclonus on acyclovir, gabapentinoid, baclofen or carbapenem means accumulation.`,`Recurrent hypoglycaemia on glibenclamide or insulin as CKD progresses.`,`**Lithium toxicity** after a thiazide, ACEi or NSAID; dialysis if severe.`]},{title:`Referral and follow-up`,points:[`Monitor creatinine and potassium **3-6 monthly in G3-4** and monthly in G5.`,`Refer for eGFR under 30, rapid fall, or non-recovering drug-induced AKI.`,`Ask about new OTC, herbal and NSAID use at every visit.`,`Report serious reactions to the **Pharmacovigilance Programme of India (PvPI)**.`]}],tables:[{heading:`Common OPD drugs by kidney function`,columns:[`Drug`,`45 or more`,`30-44`,`15-29`,`Under 15 or dialysis`],rows:[[`Metformin`,`Full, up to 2000 mg`,`Max 1000 mg; do not start`,`Stop`,`Contraindicated`],[`Nitrofurantoin`,`Standard`,`Avoid`,`Contraindicated`,`Contraindicated`],[`NSAIDs`,`Avoid; short course only`,`Avoid`,`Contraindicated`,`Contraindicated`],[`Sitagliptin`,`100 mg daily`,`50 mg daily`,`25 mg daily`,`25 mg daily`],[`Gabapentin`,`300-1200 mg TDS`,`200-700 mg BD`,`200-700 mg OD`,`100-300 mg after dialysis`],[`Morphine`,`Lowest dose`,`Reduce, lengthen interval`,`Avoid; fentanyl`,`Avoid`],[`Digoxin`,`125-250 microgram daily`,`125 microgram daily`,`62.5-125 microgram with levels`,`62.5 microgram alternate days`]]},{heading:`Antimicrobials by renal handling`,columns:[`No dose change`,`Reduce dose or lengthen interval`,`Avoid if possible`],rows:[[`Azithromycin, doxycycline`,`Amoxicillin, co-amoxiclav`,`Gentamicin, amikacin`],[`Ceftriaxone`,`Cefuroxime, ceftazidime`,`Nitrofurantoin below eGFR 45`],[`Clindamycin, linezolid`,`Piperacillin-tazobactam, meropenem`,`Colistin (only with levels)`],[`Moxifloxacin`,`Ciprofloxacin, levofloxacin`,`Amphotericin B deoxycholate`],[`Rifampicin, isoniazid`,`Ethambutol, pyrazinamide`,`Streptomycin`],[`Metronidazole`,`Co-trimoxazole, acyclovir, vancomycin`,`Tenofovir disoproxil (use TAF)`]]},{heading:`Key drugs and their renal doses`,columns:[`Drug`,`Dose and route`,`Duration`,`Key side effects / cautions`],rows:[[`Metformin`,`Max 1000 mg/day at eGFR 30-44`,`Stop under 30 and on sick days`,`Lactic acidosis, B12 deficiency`],[`Dapagliflozin or empagliflozin`,`10 mg oral OD from eGFR 20`,`Continue to dialysis`,`Genital mycosis, euglycaemic DKA`],[`Enoxaparin`,`1 mg/kg SC OD if CrCl under 30`,`Per indication`,`Bleeding; use heparin in dialysis`],[`Apixaban`,`5 mg BD; 2.5 mg BD if criteria met`,`Long term`,`Bleeding`],[`Vancomycin`,`Load 25-30 mg/kg IV, then by levels`,`Per infection`,`Nephrotoxicity; target AUC 400-600`],[`Gentamicin`,`5-7 mg/kg IV, interval by trough`,`5-7 days maximum`,`Nephro- and ototoxicity`],[`Acyclovir (zoster)`,`800 mg TDS at CrCl 10-25`,`Per course`,`Neurotoxicity, crystals; hydrate`],[`Tramadol`,`Max 200 mg/day 12-hourly if CrCl under 30`,`Short course`,`Seizures, serotonin syndrome`],[`Pregabalin`,`25-150 mg/day at CrCl 15-30`,`Taper to stop`,`Sedation, falls`],[`Allopurinol`,`Start 50-100 mg OD, titrate slowly`,`Long term to urate under 6`,`Rash; HLA-B*58:01`]]}],remember:[`Stage with eGFR; dose with Cockcroft-Gault CrCl.`,`The loading dose never changes (except digoxin).`,`Never cut the first antibiotic dose in sepsis.`,`Aminoglycosides: same dose, longer interval.`,`SADMANS: Sulfonylureas, ACEi, Diuretics, Metformin, ARB, NSAIDs, SGLT2i.`,`Metformin: full over 45, max 1000 at 30-44, stop under 30.`,`Trimethoprim raises creatinine without harming the kidney.`,`Write the CrCl and date on every CKD prescription.`]},updated:[`DOAC renal dose criteria re-checked against ESC 2024 AF guideline (unchanged doses; citation updated from EHRA 2021)`],master:{heading:`Master revision table - Drug dosing in renal impairment`,columns:[`Aspect`,`High-yield points`],rows:[[`Definition`,`Prescribing for reduced clearance of active drug or metabolite; drugs with **over 30% renal elimination** need adjustment; **stage with eGFR, dose with Cockcroft-Gault CrCl (mL/min)**.`],[`Relevant classification`,`KDIGO G1-G5 bands (G3a 45-59, G3b 30-44, G4 15-29, G5 under 15); the **four actions: reduce dose, lengthen interval, avoid, monitor levels**; is it nephrotoxic, is it dialysed?`],[`Key formula`,`**CrCl = (140 - age) x weight / (72 x creatinine mg/dL), x 0.85 female**; ideal or adjusted weight in obesity; **de-index eGFR = eGFR x BSA / 1.73**; cystatin C for narrow-index drugs.`],[`Pathophysiology`,`Falling GFR -> longer half-life -> higher steady state after 4-5 half-lives -> toxicity days later; **loading dose depends on Vd (unchanged)**; metabolites accumulate (**morphine-6-glucuronide, norpethidine, glibenclamide**); low albumin raises free phenytoin.`],[`Key history`,`Full list incl. **OTC NSAIDs, combination 'body pain' tablets, ayurvedic tonics**; last creatinine; intercurrent vomiting/diarrhoea; dialysis type and days; transplant drugs; toxicity symptoms (drowsiness, myoclonus, hypoglycaemia, bleeding).`],[`Key examination`,`**Weight every visit**; volume status; postural BP; toxicity signs (asterixis, lithium tremor, digoxin bradycardia); muscle wasting (creatinine overestimates GFR).`],[`Investigations`,`Creatinine + eGFR + calculated CrCl **written on the prescription**; K at 2-4 weeks after RASi/MRA (within 1 week of trimethoprim); **TDM**: gentamicin trough under 1 mg/L, **vancomycin AUC 400-600**, digoxin 0.5-0.9 ng/mL, lithium 0.4-0.8.`],[`Diabetes drugs`,`**Metformin full at 45+, max 1000 mg at 30-44, stop under 30**; **SGLT2i start if eGFR 20+, continue to dialysis**; sitagliptin 100/50/25 mg; **linagliptin and teneligliptin no change**; avoid glibenclamide; insulin -25% at GFR 10-50.`],[`Analgesics and CNS drugs`,`**Paracetamol safe; no NSAIDs under eGFR 30**; avoid morphine and pethidine - fentanyl/buprenorphine; tramadol max 200 mg/day 12-hourly if CrCl under 30; **gabapentin/pregabalin reduce by CrCl**; baclofen avoid in G4-5; lithium avoid in G4-5.`],[`Cardiovascular drugs`,`**Apixaban 2.5 mg BD if CrCl 15-29 or 2 of age 80+, weight 60 kg or less, creatinine 1.5+**; rivaroxaban 15 mg if CrCl 15-49; edoxaban 30 mg if CrCl 15-50; **dabigatran avoid under 30**; enoxaparin 1 mg/kg OD if CrCl under 30; digoxin 62.5-125 microgram with levels; rosuvastatin max 10 mg if CrCl under 30.`],[`Antimicrobials`,`**Full first dose always**; no change - azithromycin, doxycycline, ceftriaxone, moxifloxacin, rifampicin, isoniazid; reduce - beta-lactams, carbapenems, cipro/levo, co-trimoxazole, acyclovir; **avoid nitrofurantoin under eGFR 45**, aminoglycosides; TB - **PZA and EMB thrice weekly if CrCl under 30**; TDF -> TAF.`],[`Special groups`,`**Haemodialysis: dose as CrCl under 10, give dialysable drugs after the session**; CAPD - IP antibiotics; CRRT - higher doses; **transplant - CYP3A4 interactions with tacrolimus**; elderly - 'normal' creatinine 1.0 hides CrCl about 30 (80 y, 45 kg woman); AKI - assume CrCl under 10-20 while rising.`],[`Complications`,`Lactic acidosis (metformin), hypoglycaemia, opioid and gabapentinoid encephalopathy, carbapenem/acyclovir seizures, bleeding on DOAC/LMWH, aminoglycoside oto-nephrotoxicity, lithium toxicity, **'triple whammy' AKI**.`],[`Red flags & referral`,`OTC diclofenac in CKD; metformin in sepsis or eGFR under 30; tinnitus on aminoglycoside; **transplant patient started on rifampicin or clarithromycin**; lithium toxicity after thiazide/ACEi/NSAID; bleeding with falling CrCl.`],[`Follow-up & monitoring`,`Recalculate CrCl and **review every renally dosed drug at each visit and after any AKI or admission**; creatinine and K 3-6 monthly in G3-4; yearly structured medication review; report ADRs to **PvPI**.`],[`Prevention / programme`,`**No-NSAID card, written sick-day rules (SADMANS)**, deprescribe PPIs, avoid nephrotoxic combinations (vancomycin + pip-taz), polyethylene glycol not phosphate enemas, group II gadolinium.`],[`Latest guideline change`,`**ESC 2024 AF**: DOAC reduction criteria unchanged - **apixaban 2-of-3 rule** (or CrCl 15-29), rivaroxaban 15 mg at CrCl 15-49, edoxaban 30 mg at 15-50, dabigatran not under 30; **KDIGO 2024**: de-indexed eGFR or CrCl, **cystatin C** for narrow-index drugs.`],[`Exam one-liners`,`Loading dose never changes (except digoxin); **aminoglycosides keep the dose, lengthen the interval; beta-lactams keep the interval, reduce the dose**; trimethoprim, cimetidine, dolutegravir raise creatinine without harm; in evolving AKI every equation overestimates.`]]},flows:[{kind:`flow`,heading:`Prescribing for a patient with CKD - five questions`,caption:`Run every new drug through this before signing the prescription.`,steps:[{label:`Is kidney function stable?`,detail:`Rising creatinine (AKI) - assume CrCl under 10-20 for toxic drugs`,tone:`decision`},{label:`Calculate Cockcroft-Gault CrCl`,detail:`Ideal or adjusted weight in obesity; cystatin C for narrow-index drugs`},{label:`Give the full loading dose`,detail:`Never reduce the first antibiotic dose in sepsis`,tone:`warn`},{label:`Reduce dose, lengthen interval, avoid or monitor`,detail:`Concentration-dependent: lengthen interval; time-dependent: reduce dose`,tone:`decision`},{label:`Nephrotoxic or dialysed?`,detail:`Avoid NSAIDs; dialysable drugs after the session`},{label:`Write CrCl and date on the prescription`,detail:`Review at each visit and after any AKI`,tone:`good`}]},{kind:`compare`,heading:`Common OPD drugs at eGFR 30-44 vs under 30`,columns:[`Drug`,`eGFR 30-44`,`Under 30`],rows:[[`Metformin`,`Max 1000 mg/day; do not start`,`Stop`],[`SGLT2 inhibitor`,`Continue 10 mg (kidney protection)`,`Start if 20+; continue to dialysis`],[`Nitrofurantoin`,`Avoid (under 45)`,`Contraindicated`],[`Apixaban (AF)`,`5 mg BD unless 2 criteria`,`2.5 mg BD at CrCl 15-29`],[`Gabapentin`,`200-700 mg BD`,`200-700 mg OD (15-29)`],[`NSAIDs`,`Avoid`,`Contraindicated`]]}]},"renal-urology-proteinuria-nephrotic-nephritic":{checked:`2026-10`,notes:{sections:[{title:`Definition and key facts`,points:[`**Proteinuria** is protein excretion over **150 mg/day**.`,`Quantify with a **first-morning ACR or PCR**, not a dipstick.`,`A **PCR of 1000 mg/g** is about 1 g/day.`,`**Nephrotic**: proteinuria over 3.5 g/day, albumin under 3.0 g/dL, oedema and hyperlipidaemia.`,`**Nephritic**: haematuria with **red cell casts**, hypertension, oliguria and rising creatinine.`,`**RPGN** is nephritic syndrome with a 50% or more GFR fall over days to 3 months.`,`RPGN types: **anti-GBM (linear)**, immune complex (granular) and **pauci-immune (ANCA)**.`]},{title:`Causes and risk factors`,points:[`**Minimal change disease** causes about 90% of nephrotic syndrome under 10 years.`,`**FSGS** is the commonest primary nephrotic cause in Indian adults.`,`**Membranous nephropathy** affects older adults; anti-PLA2R is positive in 70-80%.`,`**Diabetes** is the commonest cause of nephrotic-range proteinuria overall.`,`Nephritic causes: **post-streptococcal GN, IgA nephropathy**, lupus, ANCA vasculitis and anti-GBM.`,`Mercury-containing **skin-lightening creams** cause membranous nephropathy in India.`]},{title:`How it happens (pathophysiology)`,points:[`Podocyte **foot process effacement** lets albumin leak without inflammation.`,`This gives heavy proteinuria with a **bland sediment**.`,`Sodium retention and low oncotic pressure cause oedema.`,`Urinary loss of **antithrombin III** causes clots, including renal vein thrombosis.`,`Loss of IgG and factor B causes infections, such as pneumococcal peritonitis.`,`In nephritic disease, immune complexes inflame glomeruli and rupture capillaries, causing **red cell casts**.`,`Crescents form in Bowman's space; cellular crescents are reversible, fibrous ones are not.`]},{title:`History in the OPD`,points:[`Morning facial puffiness, leg swelling and **frothy urine** suggest nephrotic syndrome.`,`**Cola-coloured urine**, low output and headache suggest nephritic syndrome.`,`Sore throat **1-3 weeks** or skin infection 3-6 weeks before points to post-streptococcal GN.`,`Haematuria **within 1-3 days** of a URTI points to IgA nephropathy.`,`Ask about rash, joint pain, haemoptysis and sinusitis for lupus and vasculitis.`,`Ask about NSAIDs, skin creams, chronic infections and, over 50, malignancy or myeloma.`]},{title:`Examination`,points:[`Record **weight**, the best measure of oedema, and BP with the right cuff.`,`Look for anasarca: periorbital, sacral and genital oedema, ascites and effusion.`,`Cold peripheries and tachycardia mean **hypovolaemia despite oedema** in a nephrotic child.`,`Look for malar rash, purpura, nasal crusting, and scabies or pyoderma scars.`,`Absent retinopathy in a diabetic with nephrotic syndrome prompts a biopsy.`]},{title:`Investigations`,points:[`Confirm persistent proteinuria in **2 of 3 samples over 3 months**.`,`A normal first-morning recumbent sample means **orthostatic proteinuria**.`,`Fresh **urine microscopy**: red cell casts mean glomerulonephritis.`,`Bloods: creatinine, **albumin**, lipids, glucose, and ultrasound for kidney size.`,`Serology: **C3, C4, ASO**, ANA, ANCA, anti-GBM, anti-PLA2R, HBsAg, HCV, HIV.`,`Biopsy every adult nephrotic, children under 1 or over 12, and any RPGN urgently.`,`No biopsy for a typical child aged 1-12; the steroid response is the test.`]},{title:`Treatment - first line`,points:[`Salt **under 5 g a day**; protein 0.8-1.0 g/kg/day.`,`Child first episode: **prednisolone 60 mg/m2/day for 6 weeks**, then 40 mg/m2 alternate days for 6 weeks.`,`Adult minimal change: **prednisolone 1 mg/kg/day** (max 80 mg) until remission, then taper.`,`All proteinuric disease: **ACEi or ARB** to maximum tolerated dose.`,`Add an **SGLT2 inhibitor** if eGFR is 20 or more.`,`Oedema: **furosemide**, aiming for 0.5-1 kg weight loss a day.`,`Post-streptococcal GN: **penicillin V for 10 days**, diuretic, no immunosuppression.`]},{title:`Treatment - next steps`,points:[`Frequent relapses: **levamisole**, MMF, cyclophosphamide, tacrolimus or rituximab.`,`Steroid resistance: biopsy, genetic testing and **tacrolimus** with an ACE inhibitor.`,`Membranous, moderate-high risk: **rituximab 1 g twice**, two weeks apart.`,`Adaptive FSGS from obesity is treated supportively; **steroids harm**.`,`IgA nephropathy at risk: RAS blockade plus SGLT2i, with **targeted-release budesonide**.`,`Lupus nephritis: **mycophenolate** or Euro-Lupus cyclophosphamide, and hydroxychloroquine for all.`,`Anticoagulate if albumin is under 2.0-2.5 g/dL with extra risk.`]},{title:`Complications and red flags`,points:[`**Pulmonary-renal syndrome**: admit today for pulse methylprednisolone and plasma exchange.`,`Nephrotic child with fever and abdominal pain: **peritonitis**; IV ceftriaxone at once.`,`Nephrotic child with cold peripheries: hypovolaemic crisis; stop diuretics, give albumin.`,`Sudden flank pain in membranous nephropathy suggests **renal vein thrombosis**.`,`Acute nephritis with seizures or breathlessness: hypertensive encephalopathy or pulmonary oedema.`]},{title:`Referral and follow-up`,points:[`Refer all adult nephrotic syndrome, **RPGN the same day**, and ACR over 300 mg/g.`,`In PSGN, **C3 should normalise by 6-8 weeks**; still low at 8-12 weeks needs biopsy.`,`Target proteinuria **under 0.5 g/day** and systolic BP under 120-130.`,`Monitor steroid toxicity: BP, glucose, growth, eyes and bone.`,`Give **pneumococcal vaccines**; live vaccines only off high-dose steroids for 4 weeks.`,`Stop ACEi, ARB, MMF and SGLT2i before conception.`]}],tables:[{heading:`Nephrotic versus nephritic syndrome`,columns:[`Feature`,`Nephrotic`,`Nephritic`],rows:[[`Proteinuria`,`Over 3.5 g/day`,`Usually under 3.5 g/day`],[`Urine sediment`,`Bland; oval fat bodies`,`Red cell casts, dysmorphic red cells`],[`Serum albumin`,`Under 3.0 g/dL`,`Normal or mildly low`],[`Oedema`,`Marked, generalised`,`Mild, periorbital`],[`BP and urine output`,`Usually normal`,`Hypertension, oliguria`],[`Lesion`,`Podocyte injury, no inflammation`,`Inflammation, complement activation`],[`Typical causes`,`Minimal change, FSGS, membranous, diabetes`,`Post-streptococcal, IgA, lupus, ANCA`]]},{heading:`Childhood nephrotic syndrome terms`,columns:[`Term`,`Definition`,`Action`],rows:[[`Remission`,`Dipstick nil or trace for 3 days`,`Continue steroid schedule`],[`Relapse`,`Dipstick 3+ for 3 days after remission`,`Prednisolone 60 mg/m2 until remission`],[`Frequently relapsing`,`2 in 6 months or 3 in 12 months`,`Steroid-sparing agent`],[`Steroid-dependent`,`2 relapses on steroids or within 14 days`,`Steroid-sparing agent; rituximab`],[`Steroid-resistant`,`No remission after 4 weeks daily steroid`,`Biopsy, genetics, tacrolimus; refer`]]},{heading:`Complement pattern`,columns:[`C3 and C4`,`Pathway`,`Think of`],rows:[[`Low C3, normal C4`,`Alternative`,`Post-infectious GN, C3 glomerulopathy`],[`Low C3 and low C4`,`Classical`,`Lupus, cryoglobulinaemia, shunt nephritis`],[`Both normal`,`None`,`IgA, ANCA vasculitis, anti-GBM`]]},{heading:`Drugs in glomerular disease`,columns:[`Drug`,`Dose and route`,`Duration`,`Key side effects / cautions`],rows:[[`Prednisolone (child)`,`60 mg/m2/day oral, then 40 mg/m2 alternate days`,`6 weeks plus 6 weeks`,`Cushingoid, growth failure, infection`],[`Prednisolone (adult MCD)`,`1 mg/kg/day oral, max 80 mg`,`4-16 weeks, then taper`,`Hyperglycaemia, osteoporosis`],[`Levamisole`,`2-2.5 mg/kg alternate days`,`12-24 months`,`Neutropenia; monthly count`],[`Tacrolimus`,`0.1-0.2 mg/kg/day in 2 doses`,`Per specialist, by trough`,`Nephrotoxicity, tremor, diabetes`],[`Rituximab`,`1 g IV twice, 2 weeks apart (MN)`,`One course, repeat per response`,`Infusion reaction; screen hepatitis B`],[`Ramipril`,`2.5-10 mg oral OD`,`Long term, maximum tolerated`,`High K, AKI; fetotoxic`],[`Dapagliflozin`,`10 mg oral OD`,`Long term`,`Genital mycosis, ketoacidosis`],[`Furosemide`,`40-80 mg OD-BD; child 1-2 mg/kg/day`,`Until oedema settles`,`Hypovolaemia, low K`],[`Atorvastatin`,`10-40 mg oral OD`,`While nephrotic persists`,`Myalgia`],[`Penicillin V (PSGN)`,`Adult 500 mg BD-QDS; child 250 mg QDS`,`10 days`,`Allergy; erythromycin instead`]]}],remember:[`Dipstick misses A2 albuminuria and light chains.`,`A red cell cast proves a glomerular source.`,`PSGN: throat 1-3 weeks, skin 3-6 weeks; IgA: within 1-3 days.`,`Low C3 normal C4: post-infectious; low C3 and C4: lupus.`,`Child aged 1-12 with typical nephrotic syndrome: steroids, no biopsy.`,`Anti-PLA2R can spare a biopsy but never a malignancy screen.`,`Maximal ACEi or ARB plus SGLT2i is the base of all proteinuric disease.`]},updated:[`KDIGO 2025 IgA nephropathy: proteinuria 0.5 g/day or more defines risk; treat immune process and nephron loss together (not 90 days of supportive care first); target proteinuria under 0.5 g/day (ideally under 0.3) and eGFR loss under 1 mL/min/year`,`KDIGO 2025 nephrotic syndrome in children: total first-episode steroid course 8-12 weeks retained (longer courses not adopted); ISPN 6 + 6 week schedule fits`,`KDIGO 2025 (aligned with IPNA): frequently relapsing = 2 or more relapses in 6 months after initial remission or 3 or more in any 12 months (was 4 or more)`],master:{heading:`Master revision table - Proteinuria, nephrotic and nephritic syndromes`,columns:[`Aspect`,`High-yield points`],rows:[[`Definition`,`Proteinuria over **150 mg/day**; **nephrotic = proteinuria over 3.5 g/day (PCR over 3000 mg/g; child over 40 mg/m2/h or UPCR 2+ mg/mg) + albumin under 3.0 g/dL + oedema + hyperlipidaemia**; **nephritic = haematuria with RBC casts, HTN, oliguria, rising creatinine**.`],[`Relevant classification`,`Albuminuria **A1 under 30, A2 30-300, A3 over 300 mg/g**; RPGN types **1 anti-GBM (linear), 2 immune complex (granular), 3 pauci-immune (ANCA)**; childhood NS by steroid response - sensitive, **frequently relapsing (2+ in 6 months or 3+ in 12 months - KDIGO 2025)**, dependent, resistant (no remission at 4 weeks).`],[`Aetiology & risk factors`,`Nephrotic: **MCD (90% under 10 y)**, **FSGS (commonest in Indian adults)**, **membranous (anti-PLA2R)**, diabetes, amyloid, lupus, HBV/HCV/HIV, **mercury skin creams**. Nephritic: **PSGN**, **IgA nephropathy**, lupus, ANCA, anti-GBM, IgA vasculitis.`],[`Pathophysiology`,`Nephrotic: **podocyte effacement** -> albumin leak, bland sediment -> low oncotic pressure + **ENaC activation** -> oedema; losses of ATIII (thrombosis), IgG (infection). Nephritic: immune complexes -> complement -> **proliferation** -> low GFR, HTN, RBC casts -> crescents (RPGN).`],[`Key history`,`Morning facial puffiness, frothy urine; cola urine, oliguria, headache; **latency - throat 1-3 weeks / skin 3-6 weeks (PSGN) vs 1-3 days (IgA)**; rash, arthritis, haemoptysis; drugs, skin creams; diabetes with retinopathy; relapse diary in children.`],[`Key examination`,`Weight, height centile, BP; oedema, ascites, effusion; **hypovolaemia despite oedema in children**; peritonitis, cellulitis, scabies scars; lupus/vasculitis signs; fundus; thrombosis signs.`],[`Investigations`,`**First-morning ACR or PCR** (dipstick misses A2 and light chains); confirm 2 of 3; **fresh urine microscopy** - RBC casts vs oval fat bodies; creatinine, albumin, lipids, USG; **C3/C4, ASO, ANA/dsDNA, ANCA, anti-GBM, anti-PLA2R**, HBsAg, HCV, HIV, SFLC over 40.`],[`Biopsy rules`,`**All adult nephrotic** (except typical DKD or PLA2R-positive membranous), **child under 1 or over 12**, steroid-resistant or atypical child, **any RPGN urgently**, C3 still low at 8-12 weeks; **no biopsy for typical child 1-12**.`],[`Treatment - first line`,`Child first episode: **prednisolone 60 mg/m2/day (max 60) 6 weeks then 40 mg/m2 alternate days 6 weeks** (KDIGO 2025: 8-12 weeks total); relapse 60 mg/m2 until remission 3 days then 40 mg/m2 AD 4 weeks; adult MCD 1 mg/kg (max 80) up to 16 weeks; **salt under 5 g**, furosemide, max ACEi/ARB + SGLT2i.`],[`Treatment - escalation`,`FRNS/SDNS: **levamisole 2-2.5 mg/kg AD**, MMF, cyclophosphamide 2 mg/kg x 12 weeks, tacrolimus, **rituximab**; SRNS: biopsy, genetics, CNI + ACEi; membranous moderate-high risk: **rituximab 1 g x 2** or modified Ponticelli; **IgAN at 0.5+ g/day: RASi/sparsentan + SGLT2i with TRF-budesonide 16 mg x 9 months together**.`],[`Systemic GN`,`Lupus III-IV: **MMF 2-3 g/day or Euro-Lupus cyclophosphamide** + steroids + belimumab/voclosporin + **HCQ for all**; ANCA/anti-GBM: **IV methylprednisolone 500-1000 mg x 3**, rituximab or cyclophosphamide, **plasma exchange in anti-GBM**.`],[`Special groups`,`Children - home dipstick, vaccinate (PCV then PPSV23), **live vaccines only off high-dose steroid 4 weeks**, VZIG within 10 days; pregnancy - stop ACEi/ARB, MMF, SGLT2i; adults over 50 - screen for malignancy and myeloma.`],[`Complications`,`**Peritonitis (pneumococcal)**, cellulitis, **renal vein thrombosis/PE** (membranous, albumin under 2.5), hypovolaemic crisis, AKI, hyperlipidaemia; nephritic - **hypertensive encephalopathy, pulmonary oedema**, RPGN to ESKD; steroid toxicity.`],[`Red flags & referral`,`**Pulmonary-renal syndrome**, eGFR falling 50% in days-weeks (RPGN) - same day; child with fever + abdominal pain (SBP); cold peripheries + tachycardia (hypovolaemia); **all adult nephrotic**; proteinuria over 1 g/day.`],[`Follow-up & monitoring`,`Weight, BP, PCR, creatinine, albumin each visit; **complete remission under 0.3 g/day**; PSGN - **C3 normal by 6-8 weeks**; steroid toxicity (growth, eyes, bone); anticoagulate if albumin under 2.0-2.5 with risk.`],[`Latest guideline change`,`**KDIGO 2025 IgAN**: risk at proteinuria 0.5 g/day, **simultaneous** immune + supportive therapy, target under 0.5 (ideally 0.3); **KDIGO 2025 paediatric NS**: 8-12 weeks first-episode course kept; FRNS now 3+ relapses in 12 months (was 4).`],[`Exam one-liners`,`**RBC cast = glomerular**; low C3 normal C4 = PSGN/C3G, both low = lupus/cryo, normal = IgA/ANCA/anti-GBM; **PLA2R can spare biopsy, never the malignancy screen**; adaptive FSGS - no steroids; relapse = 3+ for 3 days.`]]},flows:[{kind:`flow`,heading:`Proteinuria found on a dipstick - exam work-up`,caption:`From a dipstick to the right referral in five decisions.`,steps:[{label:`Quantify: first-morning ACR or PCR`,detail:`Exclude UTI, fever, exercise, heart failure; repeat 2 of 3 over 3 months`},{label:`First-morning sample normal?`,detail:`Yes - orthostatic proteinuria; reassure`,tone:`decision`},{label:`RBC casts or dysmorphic RBC?`,detail:`Nephritic - C3/C4, ASO, ANA, ANCA, anti-GBM; RPGN same day`,tone:`warn`},{label:`PCR over 3000 with low albumin, oedema?`,detail:`Nephrotic - child 1-12 steroids; adult biopsy (PLA2R first)`,tone:`decision`},{label:`Sub-nephrotic, bland sediment`,detail:`Max ACEi/ARB + SGLT2i; refer if over 1 g/day or eGFR falling`,tone:`good`}]},{kind:`ladder`,heading:`Childhood steroid-sensitive nephrotic syndrome ladder`,steps:[{label:`First episode`,detail:`Prednisolone 60 mg/m2/day x 6 weeks, then 40 mg/m2 alternate days x 6 weeks (KDIGO 2025: 8-12 weeks total)`},{label:`Relapse (3+ for 3 days)`,detail:`60 mg/m2/day until nil/trace 3 days, then 40 mg/m2 alternate days x 4 weeks`},{label:`Frequently relapsing or steroid-dependent`,detail:`Levamisole 2-2.5 mg/kg AD 12-24 months, MMF, cyclophosphamide 2 mg/kg x 12 weeks`,tone:`decision`},{label:`Still dependent`,detail:`Tacrolimus 0.1-0.2 mg/kg/day or rituximab 375 mg/m2`},{label:`Steroid-resistant (no remission at 4 weeks)`,detail:`Biopsy, genetic testing, CNI + ACE inhibitor - paediatric nephrologist`,tone:`warn`}]},{kind:`ladder`,heading:`IgA nephropathy - KDIGO 2025 dual approach`,steps:[{label:`Confirm by biopsy`,detail:`Adults with suspected IgAN and proteinuria 0.5 g/day or more`},{label:`Treat nephron loss`,detail:`Maximal ACEi/ARB or sparsentan, BP control, SGLT2 inhibitor, lifestyle`},{label:`Treat the immune process at the same time`,detail:`Targeted-release budesonide 16 mg OD x 9 months, or guarded steroids with PJP prophylaxis`,tone:`decision`},{label:`Target`,detail:`Proteinuria under 0.5 g/day (ideally under 0.3); eGFR loss under 1 mL/min/year`,tone:`good`}]}]},"renal-urology-urinary-incontinence":{checked:`2026-10`,notes:{sections:[{title:`Definition and key facts`,points:[`**Urinary incontinence** is any involuntary loss of urine.`,`It affects about **25-45% of adult women** and a third of people over 65.`,`Most Indian women never report it, so **ask routinely** after childbirth and at menopause.`,`**Overactive bladder** is urgency, usually with frequency and nocturia, without UTI.`,`Most incontinence improves in primary care with conservative treatment.`,`**Vaginal delivery** and parity are the strongest risk factors for stress incontinence.`,`Reversible causes follow **DIAPPERS**.`]},{title:`Classification that matters`,points:[`**Stress**: leakage on effort, coughing or sneezing.`,`**Urgency**: leakage preceded or accompanied by urgency.`,`**Mixed**: both; treat the predominant component first.`,`**Overflow**: dribbling from a chronically full bladder.`,`**Functional**: normal tract, but cognition or mobility prevents toileting.`,`**Continuous** leakage day and night suggests a **fistula**.`]},{title:`How it happens (pathophysiology)`,points:[`Continence needs urethral closure pressure to exceed bladder pressure throughout filling.`,`Voiding is parasympathetic: **S2-S4** acetylcholine acts on **M3 receptors**.`,`Storage is sympathetic: **beta-3** relaxes the detrusor during filling.`,`**Hammock hypothesis**: lost urethral support lets the urethra descend on coughing.`,`**Detrusor overactivity** is involuntary contraction during filling, causing urgency.`,`In obstructed labour, pressure necrosis sloughs **3-10 days after delivery**, leaving a fistula.`]},{title:`History in the OPD`,points:[`Ask **when** leakage happens: on cough or lifting, or after a sudden urge.`,`Quantify leaks, pads per day, nocturia, and score the **ICIQ-UI Short Form**.`,`Ask about voiding symptoms, haematuria, recurrent UTI and continuous leakage.`,`Take an obstetric history and ask about **prolapse** symptoms.`,`Review drugs: diuretics, sedatives, anticholinergics and alpha-blockers in women.`,`Ask about saddle numbness, leg weakness and cognitive decline.`]},{title:`Examination`,points:[`Feel for a **distended bladder**, which means retention and overflow.`,`Pelvic exam for **vaginal atrophy**, prolapse and fistula openings.`,`Grade pelvic floor squeeze with the **Modified Oxford scale 0-5**.`,`**Cough stress test**: leakage with the cough confirms stress incontinence.`,`Check perineal sensation and anal tone; rectal exam for **faecal impaction**.`,`Assess cognition and mobility in the elderly.`]},{title:`Investigations`,points:[`A **3-day bladder diary** is the cheapest and most informative test.`,`**Urine dipstick** in everyone; haematuria needs referral.`,`**Post-void residual**: under 50 mL normal, over 200 mL suggests retention.`,`**Urodynamics are not needed** before conservative care or surgery for pure stress incontinence.`,`Suspected fistula: **three-swab dye test**; a blue swab means vesicovaginal fistula.`]},{title:`Treatment - first line`,points:[`Treat **DIAPPERS** causes first, and give diuretics in the morning.`,`Weight loss if BMI over 30; reduce caffeine; fluids about **1.5-2 L/day**.`,`Stress or mixed: **supervised pelvic floor training for at least 3 months**.`,`Pelvic floor training means **8 contractions, 3 times a day**, plus 'the knack' before coughing.`,`Urgency: **bladder training for at least 6 weeks**, extending intervals by 15 minutes weekly.`,`Functional: **timed and prompted voiding** every 2-3 hours, and better toilet access.`]},{title:`Treatment - next steps`,points:[`Urgency after bladder training: **mirabegron** first in the elderly, or an antimuscarinic.`,`Review drugs at **4 weeks**; refer if two drugs fail.`,`**Avoid oxybutynin** in frail older women; trospium barely enters the brain.`,`Postmenopausal: **vaginal estriol**; systemic HRT does not help.`,`Stress incontinence failing training: **midurethral sling** or colposuspension.`,`Refractory urgency: intravesical **botulinum toxin**, accepting possible self-catheterisation.`,`**Men** with BPH and overflow: tamsulosin; add antimuscarinic only if residual is low.`]},{title:`Complications and red flags`,points:[`**Haematuria** with incontinence needs cystoscopy and imaging.`,`New incontinence with **saddle anaesthesia** or leg weakness needs same-day MRI.`,`Incontinence with rising creatinine or hydronephrosis means high-pressure retention.`,`Sudden incontinence with confusion suggests delirium, infection or stroke.`,`A forgotten pessary causes erosion, discharge and fistula.`]},{title:`Referral and follow-up`,points:[`Review pelvic floor training at **3 months** and bladder training at 6 weeks.`,`Refer continuous leakage, **residual over 200 mL**, prolapse beyond the introitus and neurological disease.`,`Obstetric fistula: **catheter drainage for 14 days**, then a trained fistula surgeon.`,`Prevent obstetric fistula with timely caesarean and the **WHO Labour Care Guide**.`,`**Pregnancy**: pelvic floor and bladder training only; avoid antimuscarinics and mirabegron.`,`Prefer **clean intermittent self-catheterisation** to an indwelling catheter.`]}],tables:[{heading:`Types of incontinence`,columns:[`Type`,`Mechanism`,`Typical patient`,`First-line treatment`],rows:[[`Stress`,`Urethral hypermobility or sphincter weakness`,`Multiparous or obese woman`,`Pelvic floor training 3 months`],[`Urgency`,`Detrusor overactivity`,`Older adult; neurological disease`,`Bladder training 6 weeks, then drug`],[`Mixed`,`Both`,`Middle-aged or older woman`,`Treat predominant component first`],[`Overflow`,`Obstruction or underactive detrusor`,`Man with BPH; diabetic`,`Treat cause; self-catheterisation`],[`Functional`,`Cognitive or mobility barrier`,`Frail elderly with dementia`,`Prompted voiding; access aids`],[`Continuous`,`Fistula or ectopic ureter`,`After obstructed labour or hysterectomy`,`Dye test and referral`]]},{heading:`Drugs in incontinence`,columns:[`Drug`,`Dose and route`,`Duration`,`Key side effects / cautions`],rows:[[`Mirabegron`,`25 mg oral OD, up to 50 mg`,`Long term; review 4 weeks`,`Raised BP; avoid 180/110 or more`],[`Solifenacin`,`5 mg oral OD, up to 10 mg`,`Long term; review 4 weeks`,`Dry mouth, retention, confusion`],[`Trospium`,`20 mg oral BD, 1 hour before food`,`Long term`,`Dry mouth; 20 mg OD if CrCl under 30`],[`Tolterodine ER`,`4 mg oral OD`,`Long term`,`Dry mouth; avoid in dementia`],[`Oxybutynin IR`,`2.5-5 mg oral BD-TDS`,`Long term`,`Most side effects; avoid in frail elderly`],[`Vaginal estriol 0.1%`,`0.5 g nightly, then twice weekly`,`2 weeks, then long term`,`Local irritation; no progestogen needed`],[`Duloxetine (stress, second line)`,`20 mg BD, then 40 mg BD`,`Taper to stop`,`Nausea; serotonin syndrome`],[`Desmopressin`,`25 (women) or 50 (men) microgram sublingual`,`At bedtime`,`Hyponatraemia; sodium checks`],[`OnabotulinumtoxinA`,`100 units intravesical`,`Repeat at 6-9 months`,`Retention needing self-catheterisation`]]},{heading:`Devices`,columns:[`Device`,`Best for`,`Key steps`,`Common error`],rows:[[`Vaginal pessary`,`Prolapse, with or without stress leak`,`Fit by size; oestrogen; review 3-6 monthly`,`Forgotten pessary`],[`Intermittent self-catheterisation`,`Chronic retention, high residual`,`Clean, lubricate, drain fully, 4-6 times daily`,`Volumes over 500 mL`],[`Indwelling catheter`,`Last resort; terminal care`,`Bag below bladder; change about 12-weekly`,`Treating asymptomatic bacteriuria`],[`Sheath catheter`,`Men with low residual`,`Measure size; change daily`,`Wrong size; skin injury`]]}],remember:[`Stress leaks with effort; urge leaks after the urge.`,`DIAPPERS: Delirium, Infection, Atrophy, Pharmaceuticals, Psychological, Excess output, Restricted mobility, Stool impaction.`,`Bladder diary is the cheapest, most useful test.`,`Pelvic floor training 3 months for stress; bladder training 6 weeks for urgency.`,`Mirabegron or trospium over oxybutynin in the frail elderly.`,`No urodynamics before surgery for pure stress incontinence.`,`Continuous leakage after obstructed labour is a fistula until proved otherwise.`]},master:{heading:`Master revision table - Urinary incontinence`,columns:[`Aspect`,`High-yield points`],rows:[[`Definition`,`**Any involuntary loss of urine**; 25-45% of adult women, a third of over-65s; OAB = urgency with frequency and nocturia, with or without urgency incontinence.`],[`Relevant classification`,`**Stress** (effort), **urgency** (detrusor overactivity), **mixed** (treat predominant), **overflow** (retention), **functional** (cognition/mobility), **continuous** (fistula, ectopic ureter).`],[`Aetiology & risk factors`,`**Parity and vaginal delivery**, obesity, chronic cough, menopause, age, prostatectomy; neurological disease (stroke, PD, MS, cord), diabetes; drugs; **obstructed labour (fistula)**; **DIAPPERS** reversible causes.`],[`Pathophysiology`,`Continence = urethral pressure over bladder pressure; **SUI: loss of hammock support / intrinsic sphincter deficiency**; **UUI: involuntary M3-driven detrusor contraction**; overflow: obstruction or underactive detrusor -> residual -> dribble; fistula: pressure necrosis -> slough day 3-10.`],[`Key history`,`**Ask routinely**; leak with cough/lift vs urge, key-in-door; pads/day, **ICIQ-UI SF**; voiding symptoms; **continuous leak after obstructed labour/hysterectomy**; haematuria, pain, recurrent UTI; obstetric history, prolapse; drugs; neuro symptoms; cognition.`],[`Key examination`,`BMI, cognition, mobility; **palpable bladder**; pelvic exam - atrophy, **prolapse (POP-Q)**, fistula; **Modified Oxford 0-5** pelvic floor squeeze; **cough stress test** (simultaneous leak = SUI); S2-S4 sensation, anal tone; rectal for impaction, prostate.`],[`Investigations`,`**3-day bladder diary**; urine dipstick (do not treat ASB); **PVR - under 50 normal, over 200 refer**; glucose, creatinine; **urodynamics not needed before conservative care or surgery for pure SUI**; three-swab dye test for fistula.`],[`Treatment - first line`,`Treat DIAPPERS; weight loss if BMI over 30, caffeine down, fluids 1.5-2 L; **SUI: supervised PFMT 3 months, 8 contractions x 3/day, 'the knack'**; **UUI: bladder training 6 weeks** (+15 min/week, urge suppression); timed/prompted voiding for functional.`],[`Treatment - escalation (drugs)`,`OAB: **mirabegron 25-50 mg OD** (preferred in frail/cognitive risk; check BP) or **solifenacin 5-10 mg**, trospium 20 mg BD; **no oxybutynin in frail elderly**; review at 4 weeks; **vaginal estriol 0.5 mg nightly 2 weeks then twice weekly**; duloxetine 40 mg BD second-line SUI.`],[`Devices / procedures`,`**Pessary** (review 3-6 monthly with vaginal oestrogen); **CISC 4-6 times/day, volume under 400-500 mL**; indwelling catheter last resort; sheath for men; refractory OAB - **botulinum toxin 100 U** (must accept CISC), PTNS, SNM; SUI surgery - **midurethral sling, colposuspension, fascial sling, bulking**.`],[`Special groups`,`**Elderly: anticholinergic burden -> cognitive decline**, DHIC - check PVR; men - BPH, post-prostatectomy (PFMT then sling/AUS); pregnancy - PFMT, no drugs; **PFMT in first pregnancy prevents postnatal UI**.`],[`Complications`,`Incontinence-associated dermatitis, UTI, falls at night, depression, social isolation, carer strain; antimuscarinic retention and confusion; mesh exposure and pain after slings.`],[`Red flags & referral`,`**Haematuria**, **continuous leakage (fistula)**, PVR over 200 or palpable bladder, **cauda equina features**, rising creatinine/hydronephrosis, recurrent UTI, prolapse beyond introitus, failed PFMT wanting surgery, OAB failing 2 drugs.`],[`Follow-up & monitoring`,`PFMT at **3 months**, bladder training at **6 weeks**, drugs at **4 weeks** (efficacy, dry mouth, BP, cognition) with repeat diary and ICIQ-UI; PVR 4-6 weeks after OAB drug in older men and diabetics.`],[`Prevention / programme`,`Antenatal PFMT, timely caesarean and **WHO Labour Care Guide** to prevent obstetric fistula; catheter avoidance; weight control.`],[`Exam one-liners`,`Stress leaks with effort, urge leaks after the urge; **diary is the cheapest test**; systemic HRT does not help, vaginal oestrogen does; **continuous leak after obstructed labour = fistula**; check PVR before antimuscarinic.`]]},flows:[{kind:`flow`,heading:`Incontinence in primary care - type then treat`,caption:`Condensed NICE NG123 pathway.`,steps:[{label:`Screen DIAPPERS, dipstick, PVR, 3-day diary`,detail:`Treat UTI, atrophy, impaction, drugs, polyuria first`},{label:`Red flag? Haematuria, PVR over 200, fistula, cauda equina`,detail:`Refer - not primary care treatment`,tone:`warn`},{label:`Leak with effort or after urgency?`,detail:`Cough stress test; mixed - treat predominant`,tone:`decision`},{label:`Stress: supervised PFMT for 3 months`,detail:`8 contractions three times a day; 'the knack'`},{label:`Urgency: bladder training 6 weeks, then drug`,detail:`Mirabegron 25-50 mg (frail) or solifenacin 5 mg; review at 4 weeks`},{label:`Failed conservative care`,detail:`SUI - sling or colposuspension; OAB - botulinum toxin, PTNS, SNM`,tone:`good`}]},{kind:`compare`,heading:`Stress vs urgency vs overflow incontinence`,columns:[`Feature`,`Stress`,`Urgency`,`Overflow`],rows:[[`Leak trigger`,`Cough, sneeze, lift`,`Sudden urge, key-in-door`,`Constant dribble, no warning`],[`Mechanism`,`Hypermobility, sphincter weakness`,`Detrusor overactivity`,`Obstruction or underactive detrusor`],[`PVR`,`Normal`,`Normal (check in elderly)`,`High - over 200 mL`],[`First line`,`PFMT 3 months`,`Bladder training 6 weeks`,`Treat cause, CISC, alpha blocker in men`],[`Drug or procedure`,`Sling, colposuspension; duloxetine`,`Mirabegron, antimuscarinic, botulinum`,`Avoid antimuscarinics`]]},{kind:`branch`,heading:`DIAPPERS - reversible causes in the elderly`,root:`New or worse incontinence in an older person`,arms:[{label:`D, I, A`,steps:[`Delirium`,`Infection (symptomatic UTI)`,`Atrophic vaginitis - vaginal oestrogen`]},{label:`P, P`,tone:`decision`,steps:[`Pharmaceuticals - diuretics, sedatives, anticholinergics`,`Psychological - depression`]},{label:`E, R, S`,tone:`warn`,steps:[`Excess urine output - glucose, HF, calcium`,`Restricted mobility`,`Stool impaction`]}]}]}};export{e as default};